pmp:ChaosMagicAuraConcentration a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Constant, semi-conscious, intense concentration on the characteristics one wishes to project"@en, "Beständige, halbbewusste, starke Sammlung auf die Eigenschaften, die man ausstrahlen will"@de ;
    skos:definition "The powers of invisibility and charisma are said to arise from a constant, semi-conscious, intense concentration on the characteristics one wishes to project."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, p. 98; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSubjectiveInvisibility a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "An aura of subjective invisibility: others prevented from noticing one's presence"@en, "Eine Aura subjektiver Unsichtbarkeit: andere werden gehindert, die eigene Anwesenheit zu bemerken"@de ;
    skos:definition "One of the classical tests of psychic ability is called the casting of an aura of subjective invisibility: no change in one's optical properties, but those around are somehow prevented from noticing one's presence."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, p. 98; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicCharisma a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "An aura of charisma or fascination, with uses in founding cults and messianic sects"@en, "Eine Aura des Charismas oder der Faszination, nützlich zur Gründung von Kulten und messianischen Sekten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The complement of invisibility is the projection of an aura of charisma or fascination, which may have application in forming sundry cults and messianic sects."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, p. 98; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAuraYieldsInvisibility a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Constant concentration on what one wishes to project is said to cast an aura of invisibility"@en, "Beständige Sammlung auf das, was man ausstrahlen will, soll eine Aura der Unsichtbarkeit werfen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The power arises from constant, semi-conscious, intense concentration on the characteristics projected."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicAuraConcentration ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicSubjectiveInvisibility ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, p. 98; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAuraYieldsCharisma a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The same concentration is said to project an aura of charisma or fascination"@en, "Dieselbe Sammlung soll eine Aura des Charismas oder der Faszination ausstrahlen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Charisma, the complementary power, arises from the same concentration."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicAuraConcentration ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicCharisma ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Aetherics, p. 98; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTransubstantiation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Transubstantiation: identifying completely in each act, and residing in the void between acts"@en, "Transsubstantiation: sich in jeder Handlung völlig gleichsetzen, und zwischen den Handlungen in der Leere wohnen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The penultimate metamorphosis. In all things one knows both that one is this illusion and that one is not this illusion. At the moment of doing one is in it, identifies completely and lives it; in the interstices between all one's doing one resides in the void, vacuous, with no mind."@en ;
    skos:note "The page carries the running head Liber AOM without a folio in the layer read; it lies between pp. 98 and 100."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Transubstantiation, p. 99; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTransubstantiationYieldsMagicalConsciousness a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Transubstantiation is said to achieve constant magical consciousness"@en, "Die Transsubstantiation soll beständiges magisches Bewusstsein erreichen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The penultimate metamorphosis is called the achievement of constant magical consciousness."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicTransubstantiation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicMagicalConsciousness ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Transubstantiation, p. 99; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicFreedomFromConsequences a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Complete freedom from the consequences of one's actions"@en, "Vollständige Freiheit von den Folgen der eigenen Handlungen"@de ;
    skos:definition "By full identification in action and vacuity between actions one is said to attain complete freedom from the consequences of one's actions."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Transubstantiation, p. 99; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTransubstantiationYieldsFreedom a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Transubstantiation is said to give complete freedom from the consequences of one's actions"@en, "Die Transsubstantiation soll völlige Freiheit von den Folgen der eigenen Handlungen geben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Knowing that by this one may attain to complete freedom from the consequences of one's actions closes the chapter."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicTransubstantiation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicFreedomFromConsequences ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Transubstantiation, p. 99; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicHalfInTheWorldCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Nothing is more ineffectual than being always half in the world and half out of it"@en, "Nichts ist wirkungsloser, als immer halb in der Welt und halb aus ihr zu sein"@de ;
    skos:definition "There is said to be nothing more ineffectual than to be always only half in the world and half out of it."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: the half-way state is named as the ineffectual one."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicTransubstantiation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Transubstantiation, p. 99; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicChaosphere a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The chaosphere: a black sphere with eight arrows, charged with life force modulated by paradox"@en, "Die Chaossphäre: eine schwarze Kugel mit acht Pfeilen, geladen mit durch Paradox modulierter Lebenskraft"@de ;
    skos:definition "The chaosphere is the adept's prime radiant or magic lamp, a psychic singularity emitting brilliant darkness, a purposely created crack in reality through which the stuff of Chaos enters, or a demonstration that belief can structure reality. It may be given a material anchor, as in figure 13 a sphere with eight arrows directed toward the vertices of a cube, coloured deepest black to hold all colours, the sphere hollow to hold objects and one arrow detachable as a weapon; the shape is trifling beside the psychic preparation. It is charged by filling it with aetheric life force modulated by paradox: the life force from any method the adept has mastered, blood sacrifice, sexual emissions, or the body's aetheric force projected in ecstatic gnostic rites; the modulation by imprinting it with contradiction and impossibility, any two mutually exclusive ideas held at once, fire and water, Nuit and Hadit, incompatible geometries, black and white, zero and infinity, many being made to serve. In a temple, initiates and students may help to formulate it, and it may then function as a god or fetish."@en ;
    skos:note "Nuit and Hadit appear only as one of the pairs of opposed principles, called imaginary metaphysical principles; no naming was entered for them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, The Chaosphere, pp. 100-101; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicChaosBreach a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "A breach into the Chaos dimension that supplies raw Chaos force and opens a door for will and perception"@en, "Ein Durchbruch in die Dimension des Chaos, der rohe Chaoskraft liefert und Wille und Wahrnehmung eine Tür öffnet"@de ;
    skos:definition "The more power put into the chaosphere, the wider the breach into the Chaos dimension and the more free Chaos energy is liberated; once made it supplies raw Chaos force to anyone near it, and it exists as a vortex or door through which the magical will and perception reach easily to other regions of existence, like a powerful magical mirror."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, The Chaosphere, p. 101; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicChaosphereYieldsBreach a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The charged chaosphere is said to open a breach into the Chaos dimension"@en, "Die geladene Chaossphäre soll einen Durchbruch in die Dimension des Chaos öffnen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Charged with paradox-modulated life force, the chaosphere is opened into the magical dimension; the more power, the wider the breach."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicChaosphere ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicChaosBreach ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, The Chaosphere, pp. 100-101; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicChaosphereYieldsAlteringHistory a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Operating chaospheres about the earth are said to hasten the change of aeon"@en, "Wirksame Chaossphären an Orten der Erde sollen den Wechsel des Äons beschleunigen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The erection of operating chaospheres at various points about the earth will tend to hasten the immanentisation of the eschaton, the change of aeon."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicChaosphere ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicAlteringHistory ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, The Chaosphere, p. 101; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicChaosphereCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The uninitiated must be kept well away from a made chaosphere, lest they become deranged"@en, "Die Uneingeweihten sind von einer gemachten Chaossphäre fernzuhalten, damit sie nicht verrückt werden"@de ;
    skos:definition "Once made, the chaosphere supplies raw Chaos force to anyone in physical proximity, so the uninitiated should be kept well away from it, lest they become deranged."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicChaosphere ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, The Chaosphere, p. 101; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicIntegralReincarnation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Integral reincarnation: carrying the spirit over whole into a new body, the final degree of mastership"@en, "Vollständige Wiedergeburt: den Geist ganz in einen neuen Körper hinübertragen, der letzte Grad der Meisterschaft"@de ;
    skos:definition "In the ordinary course the personal aether and life force disintegrate with the body and return to incarnation broken into millions of parts scattered among many beings. The adept is said to have so strengthened his spirit by magic that he can carry it over whole into a new body, and in an exceptional display of power even retain specific memories in aetheric form; by this process adepts attain the final degree of master, by the supreme rituals of sex and death in three forms, the red, black and white rites. Liber NOX says the projection of the double is the basis of deliberately carrying things over into a new incarnation, and that the god-man reincarnates at will."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Reincarnation, p. 103; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Double, pp. 63-64; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, introduction, p. 59; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicStrengthenedSpiritBeforeReincarnation a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Integral reincarnation requires a spirit strengthened by magic"@en, "Die vollständige Wiedergeburt verlangt einen durch Magie gestärkten Geist"@de ;
    skos:definition "The adept will have so strengthened his spirit by magic that it is possible for him to carry it over whole into a new body; the rites rely on the powers developed in astral travel workings."@en ;
    skos:note "The prior step is entered at the projection of the double, which Liber NOX calls the basis of carrying things over at death and which the red rite names as astral travel workings. Set at prescribed order: the ordinary course without it is described, disintegration, but as what befalls everyone and not as a consequence of skipping."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicIntegralReincarnation ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicProjectingTheDouble ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Reincarnation, p. 103; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber NOX, The Double, p. 64; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicRedRite a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The red rite: the aged male adept bonds with a young woman, makes her pregnant, and ends his life within two months"@en, "Der rote Ritus: der gealterte männliche Adept bindet sich an eine junge Frau, schwängert sie und beendet sein Leben binnen zwei Monaten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Performed only by male adepts, when the body has become very aged or damaged. The adept forms a powerful love bond with a young, strong woman, impregnates her, and in the early stages of the pregnancy, within the first two months, voluntarily ends his present existence; the powers developed in astral travel and the love bond with the mother are said to cause reincarnation into the developing foetus. Provision is made beforehand for the security of the woman and the re-education of the reincarnating master."@en ;
    skos:note "The rite prescribes a suicide on the strength of a claim no one could observe succeeding. The work's only safeguard is the provision for the woman and the child."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Reincarnation, p. 103; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicRedRiteYieldsReincarnation a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The red rite is said to reincarnate the adept into the foetus he has fathered"@en, "Der rote Ritus soll den Adepten in den von ihm gezeugten Fötus wiederverkörpern"@de ;
    skos:definition "The astral powers and the love bond to the mother cause reincarnation into the developing foetus."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicRedRite ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicIntegralReincarnation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Reincarnation, p. 103; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicBlackRite a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The black rite: the forceful entry of a spirit into an inhabited body"@en, "Der schwarze Ritus: das gewaltsame Eindringen eines Geistes in einen bewohnten Körper"@de ;
    skos:definition "The rite consists of the forceful entry of a spirit into the body of an already inhabited being, used only in peculiar and desperate situations. The author says he has been advised to say no more of it."@en ;
    skos:note "Condition of failure with its outcome: if the invading spirit fails to displace the occupant there are two life forces in one body and the victim seems to have gone mad; in any case the memory of the taken-over being remains and must be worked through. The advice to say no more is given without its source; it names no speaker for any wording and no pm:Receiving was entered."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Reincarnation, pp. 103-104; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicBlackRiteYieldsReincarnation a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The black rite is offered as a forcible form of carrying the spirit into a new body"@en, "Der schwarze Ritus wird als gewaltsame Form angeboten, den Geist in einen neuen Körper zu tragen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Among the three forms of the supreme ritual, the black rite carries the spirit into a body already inhabited."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicBlackRite ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicIntegralReincarnation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Reincarnation, p. 103; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicBlackRiteCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The black rite is dangerous and unreliable"@en, "Der schwarze Ritus ist gefährlich und unzuverlässig"@de ;
    skos:definition "The black rite is called both dangerous and unreliable and used only in peculiar and desperate situations."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicBlackRite ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Reincarnation, p. 103; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicWhiteRite a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The white rite: a foetus chosen at random in astral flight, and a sigil visualised at death as an aetheric marker"@en, "Der weiße Ritus: ein im Astralflug zufällig gewählter Fötus, und eine beim Tod visualisierte Sigille als ätherisches Merkzeichen"@de ;
    skos:definition "In the white rite integral reincarnation is achieved into a foetus selected at random by the escaping spirit in astral flight. In case friends, followers and disciples cannot locate the new manifestation and see to its magical education, it may be wise to carry an aetheric marker: at death a sigil emblematic of one's magical aspiration is fiercely visualised. It may later be apparent to clairvoyant perception in the aetheric constitution of the developing infant, or cause some recognition of or affinity with the symbol if it is met by chance in the new existence."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Reincarnation, p. 104; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicWhiteRiteYieldsReincarnation a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The white rite is said to reincarnate the escaping spirit whole into a randomly chosen foetus"@en, "Der weiße Ritus soll den entweichenden Geist ganz in einen zufällig gewählten Fötus wiederverkörpern"@de ;
    skos:definition "In this rite an integral reincarnation is achieved, the receiving body chosen at random in astral flight."@en ;
    skos:note "The marker sigil is the one element of the reincarnation rites that names an observable consequence, recognition of the symbol by the child; the work allows that it may equally not be found."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicWhiteRite ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicIntegralReincarnation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Liber Null, Liber AOM, Reincarnation, p. 104; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

#
# Liber Null and Psychonaut (Carroll), second part: Psychonaut, harvested complete
#

pmc:ChaosMagicHypersciences a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The techniques of magic as the hypersciences of the future, rooted in shamanism"@en, "Die Techniken der Magie als Hyperwissenschaften der Zukunft, wurzelnd im Schamanismus"@de ;
    skos:definition "Magic is presented not as what came before science but as where science is heading; higher physics is said to suggest a universe run more like sorcery than clockwork. In the new aeon the thrust of magical work is to make the experimental techniques work regardless of their religious or symbolic associations, and these techniques are to become the hypersciences of the future. Their origin is placed in its most developed form in shamanic cultures rather than in medieval or pagan civilisation: a strange and terrible power known before history slipped away and is now on the brink of rediscovery. The book calls itself source material for those who perform group magic or work as shamanic priests to the community, a companion to Liber Null, an encyclopedia of forty related essays to be read in any order."@en ;
    skos:note "The work sets itself against the harvesting of Kabbalistic and goetic trivia from the British Museum Reading Room. Entered here and not as a dispute: no author or work on the other side is named."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Introduction, p. 111; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicNewAeonMagic a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "New aeon magic: experiment on technique, a spirituality of magic, and a magic force moving from matter to mind"@en, "Magie des neuen Äons: Versuch an der Technik, eine Spiritualität der Magie, und eine magische Kraft, die von der Materie in den Geist wandert"@de ;
    skos:definition "In unstable times magicians and messiahs multiply, and from the alternative spiritualities a culture selects its new worldview; the wizards determine how it will manifest. The next great human advance is said to be into the psychic domain, rediscovering and expanding the knowledge of the ancient shamans. The important work now is twofold: an experimental investigation of the actual techniques as against the symbolism, since the methods are remarkably uniform across history and cultures, and the evolution of a spirituality of magic with its own flavour, worldview and philosophy. Reality is said to have been more chaotic and susceptible to magic in the past, and the grosser power over matter to be receding as matter grows more ordered, while the magic force manifests with increasing strength on the psychic levels, in the doubling of human ideas each decade, of which science is one side effect."@en ;
    skos:note "Read with the capacity it announces, pmc:ChaosMagicPsychicPowersWithinReach."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, New Aeon Magic, p. 113; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, New Aeon Magic, p. 114; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsychicPowersWithinReach a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Telepathy, clairvoyance and astral travel within reach of anyone of moderate determination"@en, "Telepathie, Hellsehen und Astralreise in Reichweite eines jeden mit mäßiger Entschlossenheit"@de ;
    skos:definition "Telepathy, clairvoyance and astral travel, once won only at great cost by an elect few through extreme measures, are said now to be within reach of anyone armed with moderate determination, because the magic force is manifesting more strongly on the psychic levels."@en ;
    skos:note "No practice is named at this place; the work's procedures for the three are entered at pmc:ChaosMagicTelepathy, pmc:ChaosMagicClairvoyance and pmc:ChaosMagicAstralTravel."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, New Aeon Magic, p. 114; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicChaoticGood a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Magic aligned with chaotic good against oppressive order"@en, "Magie im Bund mit dem chaotischen Guten gegen unterdrückende Ordnung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The new psychic awareness is said to have a subversive flavour: magic aligns itself against oppressive forms of order. It opposes a psychiatry and medicine that patch up the damaged automaton and return him to the system, preferring that individuals handle their own mental self-defence and treat their bodies with gentler remedies such as herbs; it rejects politics as the desire of some to dominate others, is anti-ideological because ideology produces repression and corpses, is profoundly opposed to religion, blamed for at least half the madness and violent deaths of history, and opposes the superstition that the world is wholly material. Against repressive order it aligns itself with a vision of chaotic good, shown in concern for individual freedom and consciousness and for all other life forms, and at the highest level as an unspecifiable feeling for the vibes generated by human thought and action."@en ;
    skos:note "The oppositions to psychiatry, politics, religion and materialism name no author or work on the other side; entered here and not as disputes."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, New Aeon Magic, p. 114; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, New Aeon Magic, p. 115; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicOperationMindfuck a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Operation mindfuck: psychological anarchy by humour, random belief, counter-information and disinformation"@en, "Operation Mindfuck: psychologische Anarchie durch Humor, Zufallsglauben, Gegeninformation und Desinformation"@de ;
    skos:definition "The chaotic aspect of new aeon magic is psychological anarchy, applied to oneself as much as to the world. Its techniques are humour, random belief, counter-information and disinformation, used to disorder belief structures. The author's example: he advocates astrology persuasively to ordinary people and ridicules it to his magician friends, so that astrology serves to disorder what people think in either direction, and he leaves the reader to choose whether this makes him a liar, mad, enlightened or aware of the ability to live almost any truth."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, New Aeon Magic, p. 115; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicInspirationAndEnlightenment a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Inspiration and enlightenment through disordered belief"@en, "Inspiration und Erleuchtung durch ungeordneten Glauben"@de ;
    skos:definition "The aim of the psychological anarchy of new aeon magic is to produce inspiration and enlightenment by disordering one's belief structures."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, New Aeon Magic, p. 115; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicOperationMindfuckYieldsEnlightenment a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Disordering belief is said to produce inspiration and enlightenment"@en, "Das Durcheinanderbringen des Glaubens soll Inspiration und Erleuchtung hervorbringen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Humour, random belief, counter-information and disinformation applied to oneself and the world are said to produce inspiration and enlightenment by disordering belief structures."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicOperationMindfuck ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicInspirationAndEnlightenment ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, New Aeon Magic, p. 115; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicGroupSynergy a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Group synergy: a synchronised working whose collective power exceeds the sum of its members"@en, "Gruppensynergie: eine synchronisierte Arbeit, deren gemeinsame Kraft die Summe ihrer Mitglieder übersteigt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Ritual structures group activity in order to generate more power than individual efforts could; in a properly synchronised working synergistic effects come into play and the collective power exceeds the sum of the individual powers. Group work also allows experiments needing more than one operator and a division of labour where some contribute abilities others lack, and it can be done as training, as research or to create effects. The chapter examines four areas: psychism, ritual, trance and dream. No technique is absolutely fail-safe, so all activities are experimental to a degree, and some so wholly that one cannot say why they are done, since if one could, the research would be unnecessary."@en ;
    skos:note "The admission that no technique is fail-safe is the work's general statement on failure; it names no rate and no sign."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, p. 117; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicRitualUniform a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Ritual dress as solemnisation and depersonalisation: the black hooded robe, nudity, the blank mask"@en, "Rituelle Kleidung als Feierlichkeit und Entpersönlichung: die schwarze Kapuzenrobe, Nacktheit, die leere Maske"@de ;
    skos:definition "Besides synchronising group activity, ritual solemnises it: putting on ritual dress and paraphernalia marks the passage from ordinary activity to something of importance. A uniform further depersonalises, reducing the weight of individual personality so that the wearers relate as functionaries of a principle beyond personal considerations. A full-length black robe with hood serves best, as does nudity, and a blank featureless mask completes the effect to total anonymity."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, p. 117; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDepersonalisation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Depersonalisation of the participants into functionaries of a principle"@en, "Entpersönlichung der Teilnehmer zu Funktionsträgern eines Prinzips"@de ;
    skos:definition "The state in which the importance of individual personality is reduced and participants in a group rite relate to each other as functionaries of some principle beyond personal considerations, up to total anonymity."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, p. 117; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicRitualUniformYieldsDepersonalisation a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Uniform and mask are said to depersonalise the participants"@en, "Uniform und Maske sollen die Teilnehmer entpersönlichen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A uniform reduces the importance of individual personality factors; the black hooded robe or nudity serves, and a blank mask completes the effect to total anonymity."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicRitualUniform ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicDepersonalisation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, p. 117; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTelepathy a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Telepathy: projecting and receiving images and commands, singly or by a group"@en, "Telepathie: Bilder und Befehle senden und empfangen, einzeln oder als Gruppe"@de ;
    skos:definition "Telepathic projection and reception is a capacity the work treats throughout. In group training it is made more effective by many operatives projecting in synchrony to a single target, and intense momentary concentration on a sigil or symbol is usually best. The psychic censor is said to edit out most telepathic communication, and to let an almost maddening volume of it through once its barrier on the robotic level is overcome. In the paradigm of higher dimensionality telepathy is explained by information moved through a fifth dimension. The author reports dreaming accurately of things that had just happened to his mother, calling an Indian dog to him by thinking intently of it, and commanding a Tibetan monk in a market to stop and turn."@en ;
    skos:note "Three conditions of failure are stated for group telepathy: a poorly synchronised mob does worse than a single projector; projecting or receiving whole strings of images to obtain a statistical result is far less effective than projecting one image powerfully; and with repeated attempts the projecting and receiving capacities become confused and distracted. The second declares the design a statistical test would need to be the weaker form of the practice."@en ;
    skos:note "Condition of failure in a reported case: the monk obeyed the command twice and at the third stopped, looked about confused and walked off on his own route. The author explains the earlier successes by the monks' receptivity from meditation. He also reports that long enforced inactivity crossing deserts and quiet in the Himalayas brought out a capacity for telepathy, and the hazards of cheap travel stimulated it further."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, p. 118; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, The Psychic Censor, p. 162; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, The Psychic Censor, p. 163; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Paradigms, p. 199; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Anecdotes, p. 202; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Anecdotes, p. 205; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicGroupTelepathy a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Group telepathy training: synchronised projection of one image to one target"@en, "Gruppentelepathie als Übung: synchrone Sendung eines Bildes an ein Ziel"@de ;
    skos:definition "Group experiments in simple psychism are mostly training exercises in telepathy. Several operatives project at the same moment to a single target; a single image projected powerfully, by intense momentary concentration on a sigil or symbol, is preferred to strings of images."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, p. 117; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, p. 118; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicGroupTelepathyYieldsTelepathy a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Synchronised group projection is said to make telepathy more effective"@en, "Synchrone Gruppensendung soll Telepathie wirksamer machen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Telepathy is said to be made more effective by the synchronised projection of many operatives to a single target."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicGroupTelepathy ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicTelepathy ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, p. 118; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSynchronisationBeforeGroupTelepathy a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Group telepathy presupposes synchronisation"@en, "Gruppentelepathie setzt Synchronisation voraus"@de ;
    skos:definition "Telepathy gains from many projectors only when they are synchronised; a single projector will be more successful than a poorly synchronised mob."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicGroupTelepathy ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicSynchronisingRitual ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Without synchronisation the group does worse than a single projector would." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, p. 118; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAstralDoorway a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The astral doorway experiment, alone and together"@en, "Das Experiment der astralen Pforte, allein und gemeinsam"@de ;
    skos:definition "In the so-called astral doorway experiment a single operator in light trance tries to divine the symbolic import of a particular image, sigil or symbol; done alone it is called little more than an exercise in creative imagination. Performed simultaneously by several participants it can become a basis for the exchange of telepathic imagery."@en ;
    skos:note "Condition of failure: performed alone the exercise is said to be little more than creative imagination in a light trance."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, p. 118; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAstralDoorwayYieldsTelepathy a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The shared astral doorway is said to become an exchange of telepathic imagery"@en, "Die gemeinsame astrale Pforte soll zum Austausch telepathischer Bilder werden"@de ;
    skos:definition "If several participants perform the astral doorway experiment at the same time it can become a basis for the exchange of telepathic imagery."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicAstralDoorway ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicTelepathy ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, p. 118; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicSynchronisingRitual a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Synchronising a group rite by cues: a presiding officer and participants on pre-arranged signals"@en, "Einen Gruppenritus durch Zeichen synchronisieren: ein vorsitzender Offizier und Teilnehmer auf verabredete Signale"@de ;
    skos:definition "Psychic activities are synchronised by a ritual in which pre-arranged signals cue particular meditations; the group may concentrate on a symbol or mantra, or be led through imagery suggested aloud by a leader. The simplest orchestration of a full group rite is for a presiding officer to perform the main ritual sequence while the participants deliver their visualisations, mantras, ritual movements and invocations on pre-arranged cues."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, p. 118; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, p. 119; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAutomaticMechanismBeforeGroupPower a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "A group rite is effective only if it runs like an automatic mechanism"@en, "Ein Gruppenritus wirkt nur, wenn er wie ein Automat abläuft"@de ;
    skos:definition "To be effective the ritual must work like an automatic mechanism in which power can manifest without distraction or hesitation."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicGroupSynergy ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicSynchronisingRitual ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The ritual is not effective; power cannot manifest where there is distraction or hesitation." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, p. 119; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicMagicalDance a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Magical dance: frenetic circumambulation about a fixed point with collective mantric invocation"@en, "Magischer Tanz: rasender Umlauf um einen festen Punkt mit gemeinsamer mantrischer Anrufung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Among the methods of raising power that also synchronise the participants, the essence of magical dance is a frenetic circumambulation of a circle about a fixed point with collective mantric invocations, balance kept by fixing the gaze on the centre point. Over-breathing, flagellation and stimulants can also be used to heighten excitement and gnosis."@en ;
    skos:note "Condition of failure: sexual arousal is said to be hard to control and co-ordinate in a group except on an auto-erotic basis, and is more often a feature of the aftermath of an excitatory rite than a means of controlled group gnosis."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, p. 118; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicGnosticLevel a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Gnosis, the level of intense consciousness in which thinking ceases"@en, "Gnosis, die Ebene gesteigerten Bewusstseins, in der das Denken aufhört"@de ;
    skos:definition "The highest of five levels of consciousness: gnosis occurs when the mind becomes intensely conscious of anything, including the ideas of self or of nothingness. It is not intent thinking, for in it thinking ceases and the object holds the attention of the mind completely. Most extremes of emotion, not only the pleasant ones, can initiate it, and so can a profound act of single-pointed concentration; terror, anger, orgasm and various quiescent meditations provoke it. It is called the very font of magical powers and mystic states and the state in which will and perception most easily reach out and touch realities beyond the mind; what can be done with it matters more than its contents. Magical training should teach entering it and acting within it, and the gnostic level is the one least protected by the psychic censor, because in it much of the mind is silenced."@en ;
    skos:note "The work's later chapters name the two roads in the same terms: intense excitatory ecstasy or extreme meditative quiescence (pmp:ChaosMagicGnosticMethods); the chapter on weapons calls gnosis the magician's primary technique, the focusing of consciousness by physiological means."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Levels of Consciousness, p. 122; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Levels of Consciousness, p. 123; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, The Psychic Censor, p. 163; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMagicalDanceYieldsGnosticLevel a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Magical dance, over-breathing, flagellation and stimulants are said to heighten excitement and gnosis"@en, "Magischer Tanz, Überatmung, Geißelung und Stimulanzien sollen Erregung und Gnosis steigern"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magical dance raises power and synchronises the participants, and over-breathing, flagellation and stimulants are said to heighten excitement and gnosis."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicMagicalDance ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicGnosticLevel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, p. 118; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicFourRitualObjectives a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Four objectives of full ritual: enchantment, evocation, invocation, divination"@en, "Vier Ziele des vollen Rituals: Bezauberung, Evokation, Invokation, Divination"@de ;
    skos:definition "Full rituals of the meditative or ecstatic kind are usually directed toward one of four objectives: enchantment, making things happen directly by magic; evocation, making things happen through the agency of demons and elementals; invocation, summoning entities and thought forms for the inspiration of their knowledge and conversation; and divination, obtaining knowledge by direct magical means."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, p. 118; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicEnchantmentPsychonaut a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Enchantment: making things happen directly by magic"@en, "Bezauberung: Dinge unmittelbar durch Magie geschehen lassen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Enchantment is defined as making things happen directly by magic, and in the Mass of Chaos as projecting spells to modify physical reality. Enchantments are said to be cast partly from the robotic level rather than from awareness, so the procedure must be performable without thinking about it; chemical agents find very little use in it. In the paradigm of morphic fields sympathetic magic produces the desired event elsewhere, and in that of higher dimensionality spell casting and enchantment are possible because transformations caused in a fifth dimension appear as effects in ordinary reality. The ordained priest must show psychic ability in enchantment by any preferred method."@en ;
    skos:note "Condition of failure: the more someone is centred in the awareness level, the more difficult magic may be for him (pmc:ChaosMagicRoboticLevel)."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, p. 118; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Levels of Consciousness, p. 122; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, The Mass of Chaos, p. 130; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Ordination, p. 142; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 150; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Paradigms, p. 194; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Paradigms, p. 199; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDivinationPsychonaut a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Divination: obtaining knowledge by direct magical means"@en, "Divination: Wissen auf unmittelbar magischem Weg gewinnen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Divination is defined as obtaining knowledge by direct magical means. Most methodical forms of divination are said to be designed to occupy the mind with a thoughtless robotic task; the cup weapon, as mirror, shew stone, tarot pack or trance, preoccupies the mind so that magical perceptions surface unhindered by discursive thought. An initiatory ordeal may test it by asking the candidate to give the history of an object presented to him, and the ordained priest must show psychic ability in divination by any preferred method."@en ;
    skos:note "The ordeal of giving the history of a presented object is one of the few procedures the work proposes whose outcome others can check against what is known of the object."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, p. 118; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Levels of Consciousness, p. 122; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Initiation, p. 134; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Ordination, p. 142; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Weapons, p. 188; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicGroupTrance a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Operator-controlled trance: a subject sent to seek information or to act past the censor"@en, "Vom Operator gelenkte Trance: ein Subjekt, das Auskunft sucht oder am Zensor vorbei handelt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Trance states from mild suggestibility to deep hypnosis can serve group magical work. One operator persuades one or more subjects, relaxed or lightly drugged, into a receptive condition by suggestion or invocation. The psychic censor is less active in trance but often blocks the awareness of magical events from reaching other levels; when another person controls the trance this can be overcome. The subject can be directed to seek information clairvoyantly and relate it to the operator, or his will can be directed to perform a magical act the censor would normally forbid."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, p. 119; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicClairvoyance a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Clairvoyance: perception without the senses"@en, "Hellsehen: Wahrnehmung ohne die Sinne"@de ;
    skos:definition "Any act of conscious perception is called magic, and sometimes perception occurs directly without the senses, as in clairvoyance. A subject in operator-controlled trance can seek information clairvoyantly; at menstruation a woman is said to be in her most clairvoyant phase; in the paradigm of morphic fields clairvoyance is the tapping of a field left by an event in the past, and in that of higher dimensionality information moved through a fifth dimension explains it. Inspiration and clairvoyant impressions often intrude into the robotic level."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, p. 119; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Levels of Consciousness, p. 122; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Time, p. 143; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, Chaos: The Secret of the Universe, p. 153; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Paradigms, p. 193; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Paradigms, p. 199; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicGroupTranceYieldsClairvoyance a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "A subject in controlled trance is said to gather information clairvoyantly"@en, "Ein Subjekt in gelenkter Trance soll hellseherisch Auskunft einholen"@de ;
    skos:definition "When the trance is controlled by another person the censor's blocking is overcome, and the trance candidate can be directed to seek information clairvoyantly and relate it to the operator, or to perform a magical act the censor would normally prohibit."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicGroupTrance ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicClairvoyance ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, p. 119; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTranceExperimentsCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Trance experiments infrequently and for objective results only"@en, "Trance-Experimente selten und nur für objektive Ergebnisse"@de ;
    skos:definition "One danger of trance experiments is that the operator's influence over the subject may gradually extend into non-trance states; another is that memory and imagination become very active in trance and begin to delude both operator and subject. Trance experiments should therefore be performed infrequently and for objective results only."@en ;
    skos:note "The second danger is a stated source of false results: the delusion of operator and subject by memory and imagination. The work does not say how an objective result would be told from it."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicGroupTrance ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, p. 119; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAstralSabbat a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The astral sabbat: participants arrange to dream of meeting at a real place"@en, "Der astrale Sabbat: Teilnehmer verabreden, von einem Treffen an einem wirklichen Ort zu träumen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The main magical experiment on the dream level. The chief difficulty of dream work is making the command to act in a particular way penetrate to the dream state; a pre-arranged ritual incantation or visualisation before sleep impresses the desire to act magically into the deeper levels of the mind. The participants arrange to dream of being together at some real place familiar to them, and either go to sleep in separate places at a pre-arranged time or sleep together in one place, where the first asleep may try to exteriorise in their dream bodies and astrally awaken the others. Sexual attraction can supply a motive to meet on the dream level, and flying ointments can assist the exteriorisation."@en ;
    skos:note "Condition of failure, stated twice: the command to act does not penetrate to the dream state; the chapter on the psychic censor adds that the censor often prevents either the command from penetrating the dream level or the memory of it from reaching awareness."@en ;
    skos:note "Flying ointments are entered at pmp:ChaosMagicFlyingOintment with the work's warning on them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, pp. 119-120; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicCommonDreamPerception a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "A common perception on the dream level"@en, "Eine gemeinsame Wahrnehmung auf der Traumebene"@de ;
    skos:definition "The initial purpose of the astral sabbat is that the participants achieve a common perception of one another at the agreed place on the dream level."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, pp. 119-120; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDreamLevelWill a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Acts of will on the dream level"@en, "Willensakte auf der Traumebene"@de ;
    skos:definition "Once a common perception has been achieved, acts of will can be attempted on the dream level in subsequent experiments. On the dream level the perception, and sometimes the will, is said to have more freedom to act magically."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, pp. 119-120; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, The Psychic Censor, p. 162; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAstralSabbatYieldsCommonDreamPerception a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The astral sabbat is said to bring about a common perception on the dream level"@en, "Der astrale Sabbat soll eine gemeinsame Wahrnehmung auf der Traumebene herbeiführen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The participants who arrange by pre-sleep ritual to dream of meeting at a familiar place aim at, and are said to be able to achieve, a common perception there."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicAstralSabbat ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicCommonDreamPerception ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, pp. 119-120; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicCommonPerceptionBeforeDreamWill a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "A common dream perception comes before acts of will on the dream level"@en, "Die gemeinsame Traumwahrnehmung geht Willensakten auf der Traumebene voraus"@de ;
    skos:definition "The initial purpose of the sabbats is a common perception; in subsequent experiments acts of will can be attempted."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicDreamLevelWill ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicCommonDreamPerception ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, pp. 119-120; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicFiveLevelsOfConsciousness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Five levels of consciousness: gnosis, awareness, robotic, dreaming, unconsciousness"@en, "Fünf Bewusstseinsebenen: Gnosis, Wachheit, Robotisch, Träumen, Bewusstlosigkeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "Whatever the relation between the contents of the mind, consciousness is said to occur on a scale of five states: gnosis, awareness, robotic, dreaming, unconsciousness. Unconsciousness rests the body and keeps it from harm in the hours of darkness; dreaming lets the mind digest experience and make emotional adjustments and is a window into the psychic dimension and the less accessible memory; the robotic state performs all learned automatic behaviour; awareness is a non-automatic response to a stimulus, provoked in some minds only by unusual events and self-stimulated in others, its degree depending basically on intelligence; gnosis is intense consciousness of anything. Activity may run at more than one level at once: the robotic level runs during all but the most arresting awareness and partly in gnosis, trance and hypnosis fall between robotic and dream, and the author suspects parts of the dream level run unnoticed in waking. Ordinary life oscillates between unconsciousness and the robotic, punctuated by moments of dreaming and awareness; the magician may strive for a new oscillation between dreaming and awareness with occasional excursions into the robotic and gnostic levels."@en ;
    skos:note "The chapter opens against every scheme of compartmentalising the mind, the conscious and subconscious included, as arbitrary, unrelated to the brain and moralistic, and against psychology and psychiatry as having left the cause and cure of madness obscure; no author or work is named."@en ;
    skos:note "The text of the layer breaks off in the last sentence of p. 123; p. 124, the end of the chapter, is missing from the full-text layer and was not read."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Levels of Consciousness, p. 121; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Levels of Consciousness, p. 122; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Levels of Consciousness, p. 123; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicRoboticLevel a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The robotic level, from which enchantments are cast and into which inspiration intrudes"@en, "Die robotische Ebene, von der aus Bezauberungen gewirkt werden und in die Eingebung eindringt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The robotic level, scorned by mystics, is said to have many uses beyond driving or walking automatically. Inspiration and clairvoyant impressions often intrude into it, and enchantments are partly cast from it rather than from awareness. Most of the world's geniuses had some robotic hobby or distraction to create a vacuum in their awareness into which something useful could come, and most methodical forms of divination occupy the mind with a thoughtless robotic task. The awareness level, though it is where methods and theories are refined, is of very little use in performing magic."@en ;
    skos:note "Condition of failure: the more someone is centred in the awareness level, the more difficult magic may be for him."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Levels of Consciousness, p. 122; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicThoughtlessProcedureBeforeEnchantment a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The procedure of an enchantment must be performable without thinking"@en, "Das Verfahren einer Bezauberung muss ohne Nachdenken ausführbar sein"@de ;
    skos:definition "When casting enchantments it is called essential that the actual procedure can be performed without having to think about it, since enchantment is cast partly from the robotic level."@en ;
    skos:note "Set at prescribed order: the work calls it essential but states no consequence beyond the general difficulty of magic from the awareness level."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicEnchantmentPsychonaut ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicRoboticLevel ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Levels of Consciousness, p. 122; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicGnosticMethods a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The gnostic methods: frenzied excitation or extreme quiescence"@en, "Die gnostischen Methoden: rasende Erregung oder äußerste Stille"@de ;
    skos:definition "Gnosis is reached by two opposite roads: extremes of emotion, terror, anger, orgasm, frenzied ecstatic excitement; or quiescent meditation and profound single-pointed concentration to a point of absolute stillness. The work returns to the pair throughout: for exorcism, as intense excitatory ecstasy or extreme meditative quiescence; for the psychic censor, as the gnostic level of quiescent concentration or ecstatic excitement; for emotional engineering, as casting oneself into frenzied ecstasy or stilling the mind; and in the chapter on weapons gnosis is the focusing of consciousness by physiological means."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Levels of Consciousness, p. 122; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Exorcism, p. 137; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, The Psychic Censor, p. 163; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Occult Priestcraft, p. 180; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Weapons, p. 189; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicGnosticMethodsYieldGnosticLevel a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Extremes of emotion and single-pointed concentration are said to provoke gnosis"@en, "Äußerste Gefühle und einspitzige Konzentration sollen Gnosis hervorrufen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Terror, anger, orgasm and various quiescent meditations are said to provoke the gnostic condition; most extremes of emotion and profound single-pointed concentration can initiate it."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicGnosticMethods ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicGnosticLevel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Levels of Consciousness, p. 122; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicThreeErrorsOfGnosisCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Three errors into which the intense consciousness of gnosis leads mystics"@en, "Drei Irrtümer, in die das gesteigerte Bewusstsein der Gnosis Mystiker führt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The intense consciousness of gnosis is said to lead mystics into three common errors: the illusion that oneself and the object of concentration are the same thing; the conviction that oneself no longer exists; and the obsession that the object of concentration is the supreme thing in the universe."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicGnosticLevel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Levels of Consciousness, p. 123; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMasteryOfLevels a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Mastery of the levels: opening the dream level, reprogramming the robotic, awareness on and off at will"@en, "Herrschaft über die Ebenen: die Traumebene öffnen, die robotische umprogrammieren, Wachheit nach Belieben an- und abschalten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Magical training is designed to open up the neglected dream level, to provoke an examination of the contents of the robotic level and add new programmes to it, and to teach turning awareness on or off at will and entering the gnostic level and acting within it. The Kia should be able to find expression at any level and be equally at home in all or none of them. The author reports that meditation first brought him only calm and later a considerable degree of control useful in many ways, and that recording his dreams opened a fantastic world with perseverance."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Levels of Consciousness, p. 123; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Anecdotes, p. 202; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicDreamRecording a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Recording one's dreams with perseverance"@en, "Die eigenen Träume mit Ausdauer aufzeichnen"@de ;
    skos:definition "In the author's account of his development he began to record his dreams and found that a fantastic world could be opened up with perseverance; he several times dreamed of particular things that had just happened to his mother, with complete accuracy. It led him to try to find his hands in dream as a preliminary to deliberate astral travel."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Anecdotes, p. 202; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDreamRecordingYieldsMasteryOfLevels a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Recording dreams is said to open up the dream level"@en, "Das Aufzeichnen der Träume soll die Traumebene erschließen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Recording dreams with perseverance is said to open a fantastic world on the dream level, which is the first aim of magical training; the author reports telepathic dreams as part of it."@en ;
    skos:note "A first-person case and not a prescription."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicDreamRecording ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicMasteryOfLevels ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Anecdotes, p. 202; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicKiaPsychonaut a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kia: the formless life force at the root of will and perception, the real Holy Guardian Angel"@en, "Kia: die formlose Lebenskraft an der Wurzel von Wille und Wahrnehmung, der wirkliche Heilige Schutzengel"@de ;
    skos:definition "Kia is the deliberately meaningless term for the vital spark or life force within us. The self is no more than the point at which the formless Kia touches experience; consciousness occurs only at the interface of Kia and mind, when Kia, equivalent to free will and perception but itself formless, touches materiality, ego, mind and sensory and extrasensory information, so that we have both and are neither. Because its root cannot be grasped with ideas we erect an ego, an image of self and Kia built from habit. Kia has no qualities, neither goodness nor compassion nor their opposites, is the void centre of consciousness and is what it touches; any motive found in it is illusion. It becomes more apparent when something is experienced powerfully, and laughter in ecstasy gives a glimpse of it. This void at the centre of one's being is called the real Holy Guardian Angel, the power of consciousness, magic and genius. At death it is reabsorbed into the life-force pool of this world, Baphomet, unless kept whole by magic."@en ;
    skos:note "Many occult systems are said to err in imagining that the Kia has a preordained or intrinsic nature, which is wishful thinking giving cosmic significance to the ego; no system is named."@en ;
    skos:note "The author reports that powerful hallucinogens showed him that the innermost core of his being held only the power of will and the power of perception, everything else added on and removable; the insight first appalled him and took years to be accepted and confirmed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Levels of Consciousness, p. 123; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, p. 152; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, The Psychic Censor, p. 162; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, The Demon Choronzon, p. 164; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, The Demon Choronzon, p. 165; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, The Demon Choronzon, p. 166; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, The Demon Choronzon, p. 167; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMagicalCombat a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Magical combat, to be undertaken with the seriousness of physical assault"@en, "Magischer Kampf, mit dem Ernst eines körperlichen Angriffs zu führen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The combat of witch doctors and sorcerers arises from irresolvable professional conflicts or as training and tests of supremacy; between equally skilled protagonists the results are unlikely to be fatal, while combat between magicians and ordinary men is as dangerous to either as between ordinary men. Magical combat is to be undertaken with the seriousness given to assault, the infliction of distress and disease, grievous bodily harm and murder. Magical attack is the direct opposite of occult healing though it uses similar forces, and constructive activities are a far greater challenge than destructive ones."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Combat, p. 125; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Combat, p. 127; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicReadinessBeforeMagicalAttack a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Who is not prepared to do harm physically will not do it psychically"@en, "Wer nicht bereit ist, körperlich zu schaden, wird es auch seelisch nicht tun"@de ;
    skos:definition "The protagonist who is psychologically unprepared to commit assault, cause disease or murder physically is said not to accomplish them psychically."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure. The prior step is the seriousness the concept node describes; the condition applies equally to close combat."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicLongRangeAttack ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicMagicalCombat ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "The protagonist who is psychologically unprepared to do these things physically will not accomplish them psychically." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Combat, p. 125; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicLigatureAndBinding a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Spells of ligature and binding, and operations to change an adversary's opinions"@en, "Bindungs- und Fesselungszauber, und Operationen, die Meinung eines Gegners zu ändern"@de ;
    skos:definition "Violence is called a very blunt instrument, and a little reflection may indicate more effective forms of psychic intervention, such as spells of ligature and binding, or operations to change one's adversaries' opinions."@en ;
    skos:note "Named as capacities and not described further in the work."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Combat, p. 125; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicRevengeCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Revenge is the most pointless motive"@en, "Rache ist das sinnloseste Motiv"@de ;
    skos:definition "Of all possible motives for magical combat revenge is called the most pointless, except as a demonstration and warning to others; violence is a blunt instrument and reflection may show more effective interventions."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicMagicalCombat ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Combat, p. 125; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicLongRangeAttack a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Long-range attack: telepathically sending the target information that makes it destroy itself"@en, "Angriff auf Entfernung: dem Ziel telepathisch eine Information senden, durch die es sich selbst zerstört"@de ;
    skos:definition "At long range magical attack sends telepathic information that makes the target destroy itself: to make a man fall under a vehicle is not impossible, to make a vehicle fall on a man is something else entirely. Long-range combat depends on projecting self-destructive impulses telepathically. Magical attack is usually done by stealth; there is little point in betraying one's intention unless the victim is highly nervous, paranoid or superstitious. The holographic paradigm explains the asymmetry by information: far less is needed to make a man fall under a weight than to make the weight fall on him, unless one can hex the crane driver at exactly the right moment."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Combat, p. 125; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Combat, p. 126; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Paradigms, p. 196; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicCloseRangeCombat a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Close-range combat: a felt force from the navel projected into the enemy's body"@en, "Nahkampf: eine fühlbare Kraft aus dem Nabel in den Körper des Feindes gesandt"@de ;
    skos:definition "At short range it is said to be possible to injure or drain an adversary's energy field with one's own. This demands close proximity, usually contact, and is effected not by mere will or visualisation but by projecting a force that can actually be felt, usually through the hands, more rarely through the voice, the eyes or the breath. The force originates in the navel area. A part of it is put into the enemy's body to disrupt the vital energies, leading to disease and death. The only defences are evading contact or having enough control over one's internal energies to neutralise the incoming disruptive energy."@en ;
    skos:note "Condition of failure: close combat is not effected by mere will or visualisation; without a force that can be felt, and without proximity, it does not take place."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Combat, p. 125; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Combat, p. 126; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicForceArousingDisciplines a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Arousing the navel force by breath, concentration, visualisation and sexual disciplines"@en, "Die Nabelkraft durch Atem, Konzentration, Visualisierung und sexuelle Disziplinen wecken"@de ;
    skos:definition "The force used in close combat originates in the navel area and is aroused by the disciplines of breath, concentration, visualisation and by sexual disciplines."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Combat, p. 125; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicForceArousingDisciplinesYieldCloseRangeCombat a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The disciplines of breath, concentration, visualisation and sex are said to arouse the projectable force"@en, "Die Disziplinen von Atem, Konzentration, Visualisierung und Sexualität sollen die sendbare Kraft wecken"@de ;
    skos:definition "The navel force projected in close combat is said to be aroused by these disciplines; part of it put into an enemy's body disrupts his vital energies."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicForceArousingDisciplines ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicCloseRangeCombat ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Combat, p. 125; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Combat, p. 126; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsychicVampirism a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Psychic vampirism, passive or deliberate"@en, "Psychischer Vampirismus, unwillkürlich oder gewollt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Psychic vampirism may be an entirely passive and unintended phenomenon, as when young persons live intimately with much older people. Vital energies are said not to be easily drawn from a weaker person into a stronger sorcerer unless the sorcerer first kills or severely weakens his victim at close range."@en ;
    skos:note "Condition of failure stated with the claim: without the victim first being gravely weakened the energies are not easily drawn."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Combat, p. 126; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicImageAttack a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Attack through the image of the target wounded as required"@en, "Angriff über das Bild des Ziels, so verwundet wie gewünscht"@de ;
    skos:definition "The long-range attack is sent through the image of the target wounded in the required manner; wax images, photographs, hair or nail parings help to connect the visualised image with the target. To focus the sorcerer's psychic energy the attack is launched from a state of deepest concentration or from a pinnacle of ecstatic excitement; hate and anger aroused during a full ritual destruction of the image may serve, and the magician may inflict pain on himself, imagining it comes from his adversary, to arouse his fury. Foremost among the ways of avoiding the dangers of the technique is to have one's apprentices do the work."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Combat, p. 126; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicImageAttackYieldsLongRangeAttack a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The image attack is said to carry self-destructive impulses to the target"@en, "Der Bildangriff soll dem Ziel selbstzerstörerische Impulse übertragen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Launched from deep concentration or ecstatic excitement through a visualised image linked to the target, the attack is said to project self-destructive impulses telepathically."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicImageAttack ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicLongRangeAttack ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Combat, p. 126; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicBlackFast a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The Black Fast: energies aroused by fasting directed at the target"@en, "Das Schwarze Fasten: durch Fasten geweckte Energien auf das Ziel gerichtet"@de ;
    skos:definition "A longer method calling for protracted concentration, in which the psychic energies aroused by fasting are directed with malefic intent at the target."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Combat, p. 126; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicBlackFastYieldsLongRangeAttack a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The Black Fast is said to direct malefic energy at the target"@en, "Das Schwarze Fasten soll unheilvolle Energie auf das Ziel lenken"@de ;
    skos:definition "The psychic energies aroused by fasting are said to be directed at the target over a protracted concentration."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicBlackFast ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicLongRangeAttack ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Combat, p. 126; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicDeathFetish a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The Death Fetish: a charged device placed where the victim will touch it"@en, "Der Todesfetisch: ein geladenes Gerät, dort abgelegt, wo das Opfer es berühren wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "A composite method of attack usable at any range: the sorcerer compounds a device to carry his death wish, foul and necrotic ingredients with something to represent the foe, ritually prepared with full magical concentration, during which he adds his own psychic force for proximity transmission; the fetish is placed where the intended victim will come into contact with it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Combat, p. 126; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDeathFetishYieldsLongRangeAttack a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The Death Fetish is said to carry the sorcerer's death wish to his enemy"@en, "Der Todesfetisch soll den Todeswunsch des Zauberers zu seinem Feind tragen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The device is said to carry the sorcerer's death wish to the enemy, at any range, combining the representation of the foe with psychic force added for transmission at contact."@en ;
    skos:note "The work calls the method usable at any range; entered against long-range attack because it acts without the sorcerer's presence."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicDeathFetish ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicLongRangeAttack ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Combat, p. 126; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAethericEntityProjection a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Projecting a purely aetheric entity across space to harass an opponent"@en, "Ein rein ätherisches Wesen über den Raum senden, um einen Gegner zu bedrängen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A skilled sorcerer is said to be able to succeed in projecting a purely aetheric entity across space to harass his opponent."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Combat, p. 126; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDiviningTheEnemyCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Divining the enemy's intention increases one's vulnerability"@en, "Die Absicht des Feindes zu erkunden erhöht die eigene Verwundbarkeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "The main difficulty in defence against magical attack is that the very act of trying to divine the enemy's precise intention increases one's vulnerability to it; a third party is most useful here."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicProtectiveEntities ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Combat, p. 126; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Combat, p. 127; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicCounterAttackCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Counter-attack is high-risk, and returning an identical attack riskiest of all"@en, "Gegenangriff ist riskant, und den gleichen Angriff zurückzusenden am riskantesten"@de ;
    skos:definition "A counter-attack on its own is a high-risk strategy once the enemy has the initiative, and sending back an identical attack is riskiest of all: preparing an attack inevitably generates self-destructive impulses for projection, which can always backfire, doubly so in this case, like a duel with grenades."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicLongRangeAttack ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Combat, p. 127; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicProtectiveEntities a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Protective entities: thought forms and entities anchored in talismans, amulets and fetishes"@en, "Schützende Wesen: Gedankenformen und in Talismanen, Amuletten und Fetischen verankerte Wesen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The most effective defences are said to be provided by sentient or semi-sentient entities. Prolonged obsessive religious activity creates for the ordinary man a minor aetheric thought form he may call his god; the effect is partly transferable, which is said to explain the difficulty of attacking popular public figures, and when such a figure falls from favour and loses the protective thoughts of his followers, sickness and death often follow quickly. The sorcerer creates his entities with more deliberation and care."@en ;
    skos:note "The fate of fallen public figures is offered as an observation in support of the claim; no case is named."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Combat, p. 127; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicEntityAnchoring a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Anchoring entities in talismans, amulets and fetishes"@en, "Wesen in Talismanen, Amuletten und Fetischen verankern"@de ;
    skos:definition "Entities anchored in talismans, amulets and fetishes are made by concentrating psychic energies in various objects, sometimes aided by sacrifices of blood or sexual secretions."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Combat, p. 127; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicEntityAnchoringYieldsProtectiveEntities a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Concentrating psychic energy in objects is said to create protective entities"@en, "Seelische Energie in Gegenständen zu sammeln soll schützende Wesen erschaffen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Entities anchored in talismans, amulets and fetishes, made by concentrating psychic energy in objects, are said to provide the most effective defences against magical attack."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicEntityAnchoring ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicProtectiveEntities ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Combat, p. 127; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicParanoiaCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "In magical attack paranoia can be the worst enemy"@en, "Beim magischen Angriff kann Paranoia der schlimmste Feind sein"@de ;
    skos:definition "In all forms of actual or suspected magical attack paranoia can be the worst enemy, and it is the height of unwisdom to enter situations where conflict is the only option left."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicMagicalCombat ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Combat, p. 127; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicFiveRitesOfChaos a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The five Rites of Chaos of Gnostic New Aeon Shamanism"@en, "Die fünf Riten des Chaos des gnostischen Neuäon-Schamanismus"@de ;
    skos:definition "Five rites based on the principles of Gnostic New Aeon Shamanism are given to cover most situations the priests of Chaos meet: the Mass of Chaos, a general rite for invocation, evocation, enchantment or consecration; Initiation, the general method of admitting candidates to inner orders; Ordination; Exorcism, for psychic infestations of persons, places or objects; and Extreme Unction, for the dead or dying body of creatures of all species. No rites are given for creating adepts or masters, for each seeker must devise his own entrance to these grades and await the recognition of his peers. Each rite is given in general form for adaptation and may be performed by one operator or by any number."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, p. 129; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicMassOfChaos a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The Mass of Chaos: preparation, statement of intent, two invocations, oath and closing"@en, "Die Messe des Chaos: Vorbereitung, Absichtserklärung, zwei Anrufungen, Eid und Abschluss"@de ;
    skos:definition "A rite of at least six parts. Preparation readies the site, raises circles and triangles, places instruments and weapons, and may administer chemical or botanical elixirs to heighten gnosis; banishings, meditation and circle dances prepare the participants. The statement of intent is formulated as simply, forcefully and precisely as possible: holding aloft the material basis, a foodstuff to be consecrated and eaten, a sigil, a talisman, amulet or fetish, the priest intones that it is our will, and adds the aim; if the basis is a sexual elixir the priest or priestess stands empty-handed, the sacrifice being of their own bodies. The Invocation of Chaos and the Invocation of Baphomet follow. The priest as Baphomet then consecrates the basis by whatever means the god wills in him, speech, gesture or something unexpected; the Oath, all holding the basis aloft and affirming that this is my will, is the culmination; a sacrament is then eaten, a sigil destroyed or hidden, a consecrated object covered and concealed. The closing may require an exorcism of the priest; a final banishing ends the rite."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, The Mass of Chaos, p. 130; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, The Mass of Chaos, p. 132; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicInvocationOfChaos a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The Invocation of Chaos: a barbarous incantation with the sigil and star of Chaos"@en, "Die Anrufung des Chaos: eine barbarische Beschwörung mit Sigille und Stern des Chaos"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Invocation of Chaos is a barbarous incantation delivered together with gnostic methods of the operator's choice. Drawing the sigil of Chaos in the air above the circle, assisted by his assistants' visualisation of it, the priest delivers the supreme animadversion to Chaos in a barbarous tongue, given with a translation in which a speaker who calls itself the Dragon Eagle of the Primal Chaos declares that it reigns, that it is first and highest, living in the first aether, the terror of the earth and the horns of death, pouring the fires of life down on the earth; the last line is said to be untranslatable. The eight-rayed star of Chaos is visualised radiant above the circle throughout, and sacrifices of incense, blood or sexual elixirs may be made."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, The Mass of Chaos, p. 130; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, The Mass of Chaos, p. 131; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDragonEagleNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "The Dragon Eagle of the Primal Chaos, speaker of the Invocation of Chaos"@en, "Der Drachenadler des urzeitlichen Chaos, Sprecher der Anrufung des Chaos"@de ;
    skos:definition "In the translation of the Invocation of Chaos the speaker names itself the Dragon Eagle of the Primal Chaos: it reigns over those addressed, is the first and highest living in the first aether, the terror of the earth and the horns of death, and pours down the fires of life on the earth."@en ;
    skos:note "The invocation is a text the priest speaks; the work does not claim it was received."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Dragon Eagle of the Primal Chaos" ;
    pm:nameRole "speaker of the invocation, a form of Chaos" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, The Mass of Chaos, p. 130; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, The Mass of Chaos, p. 131; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicInvocationOfBaphomet a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The Invocation of Baphomet: assuming the god-form, arousing the firesnake, the aeonic litany"@en, "Die Anrufung Baphomets: die Gottform annehmen, die Feuerschlange wecken, die äonische Litanei"@de ;
    skos:definition "The priest or priestess who is to assume the manifestation of Baphomet attires and visualises himself in the traditional god-form and arouses within himself a resurgence of the Chi, Kundalini or Sacred Firesnake. Other participants assist with incantations such as the Hymn to Pan, by projecting a visualisation of the averse pentagram into the priest, and if need be by the osculum infame, which the work explains as no more than breathing onto the space between the genitals and the anus, inside which the kundalini waits to be aroused. The priest completes the invocation with the aeonic litany, in which the god names itself the Great Spirit of the first aeon, the Horned God of the second, the Dark One or Devil of the third, the Hidden One of the fourth, and Baphomet of the new aeon, the god before all gods who shall endure to the end of the earth."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, The Mass of Chaos, p. 131; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, The Mass of Chaos, p. 132; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicInvocationOfChaosBeforeBaphomet a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The Invocation of Chaos comes before the Invocation of Baphomet"@en, "Die Anrufung des Chaos geht der Anrufung Baphomets voraus"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Mass consists of a minimum of six parts in a fixed order: preparation, statement of intent, Invocation of Chaos, Invocation of Baphomet, oath and closing."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicInvocationOfBaphomet ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicInvocationOfChaos ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, The Mass of Chaos, p. 130; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicKundaliniArousal a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "A resurgence of the Chi, Kundalini or Sacred Firesnake within oneself"@en, "Ein Wiederaufsteigen des Chi, der Kundalini oder der Heiligen Feuerschlange in einem selbst"@de ;
    skos:definition "The priest who assumes Baphomet arouses within himself a resurgence of the force variously known as Chi, Kundalini or the Sacred Firesnake; the kundalini is said to wait within the space between the genitals and the anus."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, The Mass of Chaos, p. 131; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicInvocationOfBaphometYieldsKundaliniArousal a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The Invocation of Baphomet is said to arouse the firesnake in the priest"@en, "Die Anrufung Baphomets soll die Feuerschlange im Priester wecken"@de ;
    skos:definition "Attired and visualised in the god-form and assisted by incantation, the projected averse pentagram and if need be the breath on the perineum, the priest is said to arouse within himself the Chi, Kundalini or Sacred Firesnake."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicInvocationOfBaphomet ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicKundaliniArousal ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, The Mass of Chaos, p. 131; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicBaphometPossessionCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Deep trance or possession in the Mass requires an exorcism of the priest"@en, "Tiefe Trance oder Besessenheit in der Messe verlangt einen Exorzismus des Priesters"@de ;
    skos:definition "The closing may necessitate an exorcism of the priest if the trance or possession has been deep: the Baphometic symbols and paraphernalia are removed, an upright pentagram is drawn over the priest, a full facial lustration with cold water is given, and he is called by his ordinary name until he responds. A final banishing closes the rite."@en ;
    skos:note "Sign of success of the closing: the priest responds to his ordinary name. The work gives no sign by which a failed return could be recognised other than its absence."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicInvocationOfBaphomet ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, The Mass of Chaos, p. 132; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicInvocationPsychonaut a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Invocation: raising a manifestation of energy for inspiration, divination or communion"@en, "Invokation: eine Erscheinung von Energie für Inspiration, Divination oder Gemeinschaft heraufrufen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Invocation is defined as summoning entities and thought forms for the inspiration of their knowledge and conversation; the Mass of Chaos performed as a sacrament of invocation raises a particular manifestation of energy for inspiration, divination or communion with particular domains of consciousness. Chemical agents are said to be useful in such receptive magic."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, p. 118; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, The Mass of Chaos, p. 130; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 150; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMassOfChaosYieldsInvocation a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The Mass of Chaos is said to raise a manifestation of energy for inspiration, divination or communion"@en, "Die Messe des Chaos soll eine Erscheinung von Energie für Inspiration, Divination oder Gemeinschaft heraufrufen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Performed as a sacrament of invocation, the Mass is said to raise a particular manifestation of energy for inspiration, divination or communion with particular domains of consciousness."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicMassOfChaos ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicInvocationPsychonaut ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, The Mass of Chaos, p. 130; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMassOfChaosYieldsEnchantment a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The Mass of Chaos is said to project spells that modify physical reality"@en, "Die Messe des Chaos soll Zauber senden, die die physische Wirklichkeit verändern"@de ;
    skos:definition "Performed as an act of enchantment, the Mass is said to project spells to modify physical reality, the sigil held aloft as the material basis and destroyed or hidden afterwards."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicMassOfChaos ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicEnchantmentPsychonaut ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, The Mass of Chaos, p. 130; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, The Mass of Chaos, p. 132; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicConsecrationAndEvocation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Consecrating magical instruments and evoking entities for later use"@en, "Magische Instrumente weihen und Wesen zu späterem Gebrauch evozieren"@de ;
    skos:definition "Two further effects of the Mass of Chaos: the consecration of magical instruments, talismans, amulets, fetishes or food as its material basis, and the evocation of entities for later use, evocation being defined as making things happen through the agency of demons and elementals."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, p. 118; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, The Mass of Chaos, p. 130; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMassOfChaosYieldsConsecrationAndEvocation a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The Mass of Chaos is said to consecrate instruments and evoke entities"@en, "Die Messe des Chaos soll Instrumente weihen und Wesen evozieren"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Mass may also be performed to consecrate magical instruments or evoke entities for later use; the consecrated object is covered and concealed at the end."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicMassOfChaos ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicConsecrationAndEvocation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, The Mass of Chaos, p. 130; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, The Mass of Chaos, p. 132; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicInitiation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Initiation without a set formula: ordeal, empowerment, induction and the unexpected"@en, "Initiation ohne feste Formel: Prüfung, Ermächtigung, Aufnahme und das Unerwartete"@de ;
    skos:definition "Initiation can never follow a set formula, since no two candidates have the same requirements, abilities and shortcomings; existence itself is a continuous initiation punctuated by death and rebirth, and beyond a certain level the magician seeks his own initiatory experiences. A candidate approaching an organised body of occult knowledge is initiated only if he survives an initial period of instruction and assessment, and the operation is never repeated at a so-called higher level; adepts who cannot wholly satisfy themselves with the candidate and take him into their confidence have no business initiating. The formal initiation contains four elements: an ordeal to test fortitude, devotion and abilities; an empowerment with secrets, powers and knowledge of the Order, for which the candidate may be prepared by fasting, meditation and if need be elixirs and then shown forces and entities the officers conjure, led through visions in trance, taken through group rites of altered states, taught techniques or given a consecrated instrument; an induction informing him of the requirements of secrecy, with blood, nail parings, spittle and the measure, a cord of his exact height, given in pledge; and the unexpected."@en ;
    skos:note "The work sets itself against orders that initiate by set formula and against orders within orders, which exist only to manufacture hierarchy; no order is named."@en ;
    skos:note "The prior period of instruction and assessment is entered in the definition and not as a presupposition, since the work describes it only by name."@en ;
    skos:note "The majority of the rite is said to test the Order's magical and organisational abilities, the unexpected its creativity."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Initiation, p. 133; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Initiation, p. 134; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Initiation, p. 135; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicInitiatoryOrdeals a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Initiatory ordeals: the journey, guarding a station, magical defence, and tests of ability"@en, "Initiatorische Prüfungen: die Reise, das Wachestehen, die magische Verteidigung und Fähigkeitsproben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Examples of ordeals. The initiatory journey: the candidate is sent or led on a journey, through a forest at night or across a crowded city, and challenged at various points by guardians, some of whom he will not recognise as such, each making a demand he must satisfy before being passed on. Guarding a station: he is assigned a point, a tree in a wood or a lamp post in a public place, that he must not quit on pain of failure, while experiences forcing him to confront his fears and desires are arranged to tempt him from it. Magical defence: a circle is drawn around him with all other officers outside; no person may cross it and no physical weapon transit it, there are no other rules, and combat ends when the candidate submits or the presiding officer is satisfied. Ordeals may also test specific abilities: the candidate gives the history of an object presented to him, or conjures a pentagram with enough force to be seen by others present."@en ;
    skos:note "Condition of failure, stated: in guarding a station, quitting the post is failure."@en ;
    skos:note "Sign of success proposed as a test by the work itself: a pentagram conjured with sufficient force that others present can see it. Its outcome is decided by witnesses and not by the operator."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Initiation, p. 133; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Initiation, p. 134; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicVisiblePentagram a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Conjuring a pentagram with force enough to be seen by others"@en, "Ein Pentagramm mit so viel Kraft beschwören, dass andere es sehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Among the abilities an initiatory ordeal may test, the candidate may be asked to conjure a pentagram with sufficient force that it can be seen by others present."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Initiation, p. 134; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicInitiatoryOrdealsYieldVisiblePentagram a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The ordeal of ability presupposes and tests a pentagram visible to others"@en, "Die Fähigkeitsprobe setzt ein für andere sichtbares Pentagramm voraus und prüft es"@de ;
    skos:definition "The work names the conjuration of a pentagram visible to others as an ability an ordeal may test, and so claims it as attainable."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a yielding because the ordeal is the only procedure in the work that names the capacity; strictly the ordeal tests and does not train it."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicInitiatoryOrdeals ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicVisiblePentagram ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Initiation, p. 134; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTheUnexpected a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The unexpected: an engineered event that overturns the candidate's expectations"@en, "Das Unerwartete: ein herbeigeführtes Ereignis, das die Erwartungen des Kandidaten umstürzt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Order should be capable of engineering some event that greatly surprises the candidate, overturns his expectations and forces him to think or act wholly against his normal behaviour; the practical joke is called a secular survival of initiation and a reflection of the cosmic joke played out in all our existences. In past aeons this was usually a simulated death and rebirth; variants are hypnosis, hallucinogens, or temporary reduction to total vulnerability. Of what it might consist the author says no more."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Initiation, p. 133; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Initiation, p. 134; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Initiation, p. 135; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicInitiatoryExtremesCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Terror, privation and ecstatic collapse to be used with prudence"@en, "Schrecken, Entbehrung und ekstatischer Zusammenbruch mit Vorsicht anzuwenden"@de ;
    skos:definition "Terror, physical privation, or ecstatic excitement leading to collapse should be employed in initiation with a certain prudence."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicTheUnexpected ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Initiation, p. 134; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicExorcismOfPersons a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Exorcism of persons: casting out an obsession, built up or attracted"@en, "Exorzismus an Personen: eine Besessenheit austreiben, ob aufgebaut oder angezogen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Exorcism is of places or objects and of persons, a possible third kind, of animals, being rare, extraordinarily difficult and seldom worth attempting. The exorcism of persons does not presuppose that an external entity has taken over the mind: we can manufacture our own demons through bad mental habits or under peculiar stress. Many forms of possession have a sexual or traumatic component, the mental energy of arousal or trauma being diverted to feed an obsession until it grows into an independent entity or attracts one; the rite brings the candidate through a great catharsis in which his consciousness can be reprogrammed to reject the entity built up."@en ;
    skos:note "Two conditions of failure stated together: exorcism as a cure for madness is worth attempting only if the patient complains of infestation by a seemingly independent entity, and it is only likely to succeed if the possession began comparatively recently; as the witch doctors say, a long-term case, a man with a bad soul, cannot usually be helped."@en ;
    skos:note "The work sets psychiatry's carrot and stick against it: kindness sometimes effective over a long time, and electricity, scalpel and hypodermic of questionable effectiveness; no author or work is named."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Exorcism, p. 136; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Exorcism, p. 137; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicReligiousExorcism a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Conventional religious exorcism: replacing one obsession with a larger one"@en, "Herkömmlicher religiöser Exorzismus: eine Besessenheit durch eine größere ersetzen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The conventional religious form seeks to replace one obsession with a larger and more powerful one by invoking a god to cast out the demon. The exorcist must invoke and to some extent personify the candidate's god with all its symbolic words, actions and paraphernalia, then command the candidate to abandon his obsession, if necessary by quite forceful behaviour; a show of actually removing the demon, often with symbolic trickery, may be useful."@en ;
    skos:note "Condition of failure: it can only work if the candidate was brought up to fear or revere a god."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Exorcism, p. 136; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicShamanicExorcism a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Freestyle shamanic exorcism: the exorcist personifies the candidate's demon"@en, "Freier schamanischer Exorzismus: der Exorzist verkörpert den Dämon des Kandidaten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Instead of invoking the candidate's god the exorcist invokes and personifies the candidate's demon, which may suit a non-religious candidate. After observing him for some time the exorcist takes him to a secure place, ensures he is at his complete mercy, and leads him through a tour of his private hell, with foul and acrid incense, weird flashing light and smoke, behaving in a weird and threatening manner and throwing back at him all the peculiarities he has shown. The exorcist terrorises the candidate back to normality by showing him how far down the slope he has slipped and how much further he might go."@en ;
    skos:note "The author's own case: with a neighbour he calls Ron, whom most doctors would have called a paranoid schizophrenic, the standard procedures of exorcism failed, Ron mumbling and unable to be made to manifest his demons; the author then became his demon, robed in a dark room full of burning sulphur, menacing him with weapons and holding his soul at swordpoint. Ron opened up and became lucid and reasonable, and stayed normal for about twenty minutes before slipping back, and left completely crazy. Counted as one sign of success, the lucidity, and two conditions of failure, the failure of the standard procedures and the relapse; the author repeats the witch doctors' saying that a long-term case often proves intractable."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Exorcism, p. 136; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Exorcism, p. 137; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicShamanicExorcismYieldsExorcismOfPersons a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Terrorising the candidate through his private hell is said to return him to normality"@en, "Den Kandidaten durch seine private Hölle zu schrecken soll ihn zur Normalität zurückbringen"@de ;
    skos:definition "By personifying the candidate's demon and throwing back at him his own peculiarities, the exorcist is said to terrorise him back to normality."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicShamanicExorcism ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicExorcismOfPersons ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Exorcism, p. 137; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicGnosticMethodsYieldExorcismOfPersons a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Gnostic excitation or quiescence is said to be effective in exorcism, by making the mind hypersuggestible"@en, "Gnostische Erregung oder Stille soll im Exorzismus wirksam sein, weil sie den Geist überaus beeinflussbar macht"@de ;
    skos:definition "Intense excitatory ecstasy and extreme meditative quiescence are called effective in the exorcism of persons, because in either condition the mind becomes hypersuggestible, which also explains their use in brainwashing."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicGnosticMethods ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicExorcismOfPersons ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Exorcism, p. 137; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicExultationBeforeBanishingPossession a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Sexual or traumatic possession yields only to a return to a like exultation"@en, "Sexuelle oder traumatische Besessenheit weicht nur einer Rückkehr in eine gleiche Erhebung"@de ;
    skos:definition "It is only by returning to a similar level of mental exultation, by various physiological means, that possessions and obsessions with a sexual or traumatic component can be challenged and banished."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicExorcismOfPersons ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicGnosticMethods ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Such forms of possession and obsession cannot be challenged and banished." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Exorcism, p. 137; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicBreedingFamiliars a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Breeding one's own familiars, elementals and demons from diverted sexual or traumatic energy"@en, "Eigene Familiare, Elementare und Dämonen aus umgelenkter sexueller oder traumatischer Energie züchten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The mental energy of sexual arousal or of traumatic experience can become diverted to feed an obsession until it grows into an independent entity or attracts one; this is called one of the easiest methods by which the magician can breed his own familiars, elementals and demons."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Exorcism, p. 137; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicShamExorcism a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The sham exorcism of a place, aimed at its inhabitants"@en, "Der Scheinexorzismus eines Ortes, der auf seine Bewohner zielt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The first kind of exorcism of places is a sham performed in the presence of people who themselves need exorcism but cannot accept it, where the problem plainly lies in the inhabitants and not the place. The exorcist makes some pretence of dealing with the place and asks the affected persons to assist; since they have already exteriorised the obsessing force imaginatively, or sometimes objectively as with poltergeists, the next step is for them to take control of it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Exorcism, p. 137; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Exorcism, p. 138; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicObjectivePhenomenaCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Objective phenomena do not prove extra-human entities"@en, "Objektive Erscheinungen beweisen keine außermenschlichen Wesen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Where there are objective psychic phenomena, materialisations, noises, sudden falls in temperature or objects hurled about, the exorcist should not at once conclude that wholly extra-human entities are present, since humans can produce such manifestations without knowing it."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicShamExorcism ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Exorcism, p. 137; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Exorcism, p. 138; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicCoercionOfSpirits a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Coercing spirits by ordered, low-entropy structures and trapping them in crystals"@en, "Geister durch geordnete, entropiearme Strukturen zwingen und in Kristallen fangen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Spirits are said to be coerced by anything with a highly ordered, low-entropy structure: the focused human will, magnetised iron, crystals, and to some extent very pure water. A place or object found to be infested offers the chance of entrapment instead of banishing, and crystals are by far the most effective spirit traps."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Exorcism, p. 138; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicSpiritEntrapment a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Stalking and entrapping an entity in a crystal"@en, "Ein Wesen aufspüren und in einem Kristall fangen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician begins stalking the entity by giving it some basis of manifestation, darkness, incense smoke or hallucinogens according to his clairvoyant ability, to catch an impression of what he is dealing with. The rite of entrapment centres on a spirit trap, best a crystal: salt crystals are used half-knowingly in religious rites, but shamans prefer larger and more stable crystals, particularly quartz. Entities may be coerced into crystals by plunging the crystal into the space the spirit occupies, if that can be determined, or by strongly focused will aided by prods and passes with sharp magnetised iron instruments, particularly if the operation is concentrated by a ritual."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Exorcism, p. 138; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSpiritEntrapmentYieldsCoercionOfSpirits a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Crystal and magnetised iron are said to coerce an entity into the trap"@en, "Kristall und magnetisiertes Eisen sollen ein Wesen in die Falle zwingen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Plunging a crystal into the space of the spirit, or focused will with passes of magnetised iron concentrated by ritual, is said to coerce the entity into the crystal."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicSpiritEntrapment ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicCoercionOfSpirits ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Exorcism, p. 138; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSaltCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Salt picks up psychic debris, and raw salt harms the body"@en, "Salz nimmt seelischen Schutt auf, und rohes Salz schadet dem Körper"@de ;
    skos:definition "Ordinary salt is said to be so good at picking up bits of random low-level psychic debris that many witches and mystics refuse to eat it; consuming raw salt is also called somewhat injurious to physical health."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicSpiritEntrapment ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Exorcism, p. 138; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicBanishmentOfEntities a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Banishing an entity, by force or after entrapment, with water"@en, "Ein Wesen bannen, mit Gewalt oder nach dem Fangen, mit Wasser"@de ;
    skos:definition "Banishment may be done by simply forcing the entity to leave, or after entrapment. Water is called a very anomalous substance, not wholly homogeneous or random at the molecular level but possessed of a delicate structure sensitive to heat, radiation and psychic ambience; it receives a psychic charge readily and dissipates it equally readily."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Exorcism, p. 138; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicWaterBanishing a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Dissolving infested salt crystals in water, or splashing charged water about the entity"@en, "Befallene Salzkristalle in Wasser lösen oder geladenes Wasser um das Wesen sprengen"@de ;
    skos:definition "In rites of banishing, infested salt crystals are usually dissolved in water for a few days. A variation on direct banishing is to charge a sample of water and splash it around in the vicinity of the entity for its disintegrating effect."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Exorcism, p. 138; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Exorcism, p. 139; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicWaterBanishingYieldsBanishmentOfEntities a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Water is said to dissipate the charge of an entity"@en, "Wasser soll die Ladung eines Wesens zerstreuen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Because water receives and dissipates a psychic charge readily, dissolving infested salt in it, or splashing charged water near the entity, is said to banish or disintegrate the entity."@en ;
    skos:note "Entities that are the coherent psychic remains of human dead are referred to the forms of unction of the next section (pmp:ChaosMagicExtremeUnction)."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicWaterBanishing ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicBanishmentOfEntities ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Exorcism, p. 138; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Exorcism, p. 139; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicThreePartsAtDeath a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kia, aetheric body and physical body at death"@en, "Kia, ätherischer Leib und physischer Leib im Tod"@de ;
    skos:definition "Death is a precondition of life, neither possible nor desirable to prevent, and may be a great initiation or a random catastrophe that wastes the fruits of an incarnation. At death three parts of a being are considered. The physical body degrades. The aetheric body, sometimes called the soul, holds an image of the body and some of its most powerful memories, including an emotionally charged death; it begins to degrade as the body disintegrates, giving the dying quasi-religious experiences in which each may briefly visit the heaven or hell he expects, may appear as a ghost or attach in parts to places, objects or rarely people, and usually dissipates into the aetheric background after a few days. The Kia is destined to be reabsorbed into the life-force pool of this world, known as Baphomet: union with God to the mystic, being devoured by the Devil to the sorcerer, who seeks to avoid it; absorption explodes the Kia into fragments from which new Kias form."@en ;
    skos:note "Burial in sealed boxes and embalming are called revolting habits of religions truly afraid of death; no religion is named."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Extreme Unction: The Final Enchantment, p. 140; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicWholeReincarnation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Reincarnating the Kia whole, with or without some of its memories"@en, "Das Kia ganz wiederverkörpern, mit oder ohne einen Teil seiner Erinnerungen"@de ;
    skos:definition "By magical means the Kia can be made to reincarnate whole without losing its integrity; such a reincarnation is unconscious and preserves no memories. Other techniques allow the Kia to carry some aetheric with it, so that at least some of the main lessons and memories of one incarnation are kept for the next. The magician must decide for himself what course to take for his own soul."@en ;
    skos:note "The work names the capacity and does not describe the magical means or the other techniques."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Extreme Unction: The Final Enchantment, p. 140; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Extreme Unction: The Final Enchantment, p. 141; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicExtremeUnction a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Extreme unction: guiding the dying or dead through the otherworld"@en, "Letzte Ölung: Sterbende oder Tote durch die andere Welt geleiten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Present at or shortly after the death of any creature, the magician may act as psychopomp. Instruction and encouragement may be given aloud to the dying; to the comatose, the dead, or beings of other races or species, by telepathic visualisation alone. The essential points, in whatever form the candidate can understand: be without fear as the great change begins; terrifying visions are illusory, laugh at them, reject them and go beyond; you will come to the secret of your being, as a dazzling brilliance, an awesome darkness or both; it is your choice to become one with this source or to remain separate; if you would remain separate you must seek new life, and in seeking rebirth seek love, vitality and intelligence, strength and freedom."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Extreme Unction: The Final Enchantment, p. 141; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsychopomp a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The psychopomp, guide of souls through the otherworld"@en, "Der Seelengeleiter durch die andere Welt"@de ;
    skos:definition "When present at or shortly after the death of any creature the magician has the opportunity to assist as psychopomp, the guide of souls through the otherworld, reaching even the dead and non-human beings by telepathic visualisation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Extreme Unction: The Final Enchantment, p. 141; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicExtremeUnctionYieldsPsychopomp a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Extreme unction is said to guide the soul of the dying through the otherworld"@en, "Die letzte Ölung soll die Seele des Sterbenden durch die andere Welt geleiten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The message of extreme unction, given aloud or by telepathic visualisation, is said to guide the dying or dead being through the visions of death to the choice between union and separate rebirth."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicExtremeUnction ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsychopomp ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Extreme Unction: The Final Enchantment, p. 141; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicUnctionDemiseCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The demise of the being given unction must not have been brought about for the purpose"@en, "Der Tod des gesalbten Wesens darf nicht zu diesem Zweck herbeigeführt worden sein"@de ;
    skos:definition "Among the abilities a candidate for ordination must demonstrate is the administration of extreme unction to some being whose demise must certainly not have been brought about for this purpose."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicExtremeUnction ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Ordination, p. 142; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMagicalPriesthood a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The magical priest and the eight abilities of an ordained priest of Chaos"@en, "Der magische Priester und die acht Fähigkeiten eines geweihten Priesters des Chaos"@de ;
    skos:definition "A magical priest, as distinct from an adept, can administer the sacraments and rites of initiation, exorcism, extreme unction and the Mass, and discourse wisely on mysticism and magic to whoever requires it; most adepts can function as priests unless they follow a particularly solitary path. The Order recognises as ordained priests of Chaos those who can demonstrate: administration of the Mass of Chaos for invocation, enchantment and consecration; the exorcism of places and persons to effect; administration of extreme unction; the design and performance of an initiation as principal officer; the construction and use of magical weapons; psychic ability in enchantment and divination by any preferred method; the ability to enter at least one state of altered consciousness or gnosis at will; and the ability to discourse widely, wisely, convincingly and with authority on magic and mysticism."@en ;
    skos:note "The abilities are to be tested over a period of time and demonstrated to peers; the work sets no criterion by which a demonstration counts as failed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Ordination, p. 142; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicOrdination a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Ordination by demonstrated ability and the Mass performed for one's own inspiration"@en, "Weihe durch nachgewiesene Fähigkeit und die für die eigene Inspiration gefeierte Messe"@de ;
    skos:definition "Ordination is not conferred by a passive seal of approval but given in recognition of abilities demonstrated to one's peers and tested over a period of time. After satisfactory accomplishment the candidate proceeds to the Rite of Ordination proper: he performs the Mass of Chaos for his own magical inspiration and to consecrate his priestly instruments, with as many fellow officers of the Order present as possible to add their powers to the rite."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Ordination, p. 142; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAbilitiesBeforeOrdination a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The priestly abilities are demonstrated before the Rite of Ordination"@en, "Die priesterlichen Fähigkeiten werden vor dem Ritus der Weihe nachgewiesen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The abilities have to be tested over a period of time, and only after satisfactory accomplishment does the candidate proceed to the Rite of Ordination proper."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicOrdination ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicMagicalPriesthood ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Ordination, p. 142; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAdepthood a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Adepthood, entered by one's own devising and recognised by peers"@en, "Adeptschaft, nach eigenem Entwurf betreten und von Gleichrangigen anerkannt"@de ;
    skos:definition "No rites are given for the creation of adepts or masters: each seeker must devise his own entrance into these grades and await the recognition of his peers, and there is no fixed route by which one becomes adept or master automatically. Acquiring the powers of a magical priest is said to do much to further an initiate's progress toward adepthood. Performing ritual by imagination alone, the empty-hand technique, is called the mark of an adept, and quantities of a drug that move the untrained only to mild euphoria or nausea may suffice for the adept to enter trance or ecstasy."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, p. 129; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Initiation, p. 133; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Ordination, p. 142; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Weapons, p. 189; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 148; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicOrdinationYieldsAdepthood a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Acquiring the powers of a magical priest is said to further progress toward adepthood"@en, "Die Kräfte eines magischen Priesters zu erwerben soll den Weg zur Adeptschaft fördern"@de ;
    skos:definition "Initiates are said to find that acquiring the powers of a magical priest does much to further their progress toward adepthood."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicOrdination ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicAdepthood ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Ordination, p. 142; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicLunarInfluence a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sun and moon as the bodies that matter, and the full moon as the time all magic works better"@en, "Sonne und Mond als die maßgeblichen Körper, und der Vollmond als Zeit, in der alle Magie besser wirkt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The celestial bodies with the greatest physical and psychic effects on earth are the sun and moon; the other planets act minutely and indirectly, by affecting the sun, and are unrelated to attributions to antique gods. Moonlight changes its properties in bouncing off the moon, and strong moonlight exerts a general psychophysical energising effect on plants and animals; mental hospitals are said to have become bedlams at the full moon before tranquillisers. Against authorities who hold the full moon to be for beneficent magic and the dark moon for malignant sorcery, the work holds this only partly true: all magic works somewhat better at the full moon, as there is more psychic energy about, while harmful magic tends to have worse effects on its victims at the dark of the moon, when everyone is at a lower ebb, though the attack must then be made from lower energy too."@en ;
    skos:note "Astrology is said to have become hopelessly vague by enlarging itself indiscriminately; the authorities on the lunar division are not named. Entered at the claim rather than as disputes."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
