pmc:ChaosMagicMoonbathingCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Unchannelled lunar energy can intoxicate and derange"@en, "Ungelenkte Mondenergie kann berauschen und verwirren"@de ;
    skos:definition "If the energy of moonbathing is not channelled into something useful it can tend to intoxicate and derange the moonwatcher."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicMoonbathing ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Time, p. 143; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMenstrualPsychicPower a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The psychic power and clairvoyance of a woman at menses"@en, "Die seelische Kraft und das Hellsehen einer Frau während der Menstruation"@de ;
    skos:definition "Away from civilisation the menstrual cycle is said to synchronise with the moon, bleeding tending to fall at the dark of the moon and sometimes at the full, ovulation at the opposite phase. At the time of menses a woman is said to be in her most psychically powerful and clairvoyant phase. Many religious and magical systems therefore fear the power of women at this time and exclude them from temples or intercourse, while some secretive orders have used women's extra psychic powers when menses fall at the full or dark of the moon, for good or ill respectively; women menstruating at the dark moon are the exception to the lower energy of attack at that time."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Time, p. 143; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Time, p. 144; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicLunarSynchronisation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Synchronising menses with the chosen lunar phase"@en, "Die Menstruation mit der gewählten Mondphase in Einklang bringen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Oral contraceptives are said to offer a simple method of synchronising menstruation with either phase of the moon as desired, so that the extra psychic power of the menses falls at the full or the dark moon."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Time, p. 144; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicLunarSynchronisationYieldsMenstrualPsychicPower a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Timing the menses to a lunar phase is said to place the woman's extra psychic power at that phase"@en, "Die Menstruation auf eine Mondphase zu legen soll die zusätzliche seelische Kraft der Frau in diese Phase legen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Synchronising the menses with the full or dark moon is said to let the extra psychic power of that phase be used for good or ill respectively, as some arcane orders have done."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicLunarSynchronisation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicMenstrualPsychicPower ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Time, p. 144; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicFourInTheMorning a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Four in the morning as the best time for magic to affect others"@en, "Vier Uhr morgens als beste Zeit für Magie, die auf andere wirken soll"@de ;
    skos:definition "The best time to perform any magic to affect others is said to be four in the morning local time, when the body-mind is at its lowest physiological level, the time of dream, and the time when most people are born and most will die."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Time, p. 144; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSeasonalCurrents a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Spring and autumn currents: May Eve for healing and love, All Hallows Eve for necromancy"@en, "Strömungen von Frühling und Herbst: Walpurgisnacht für Heilung und Liebe, Allerheiligenabend für Nekromantie"@de ;
    skos:definition "The yearly rhythms of sex, growth, death and decay in temperate latitudes are said to create a psychic current, which probably explains most sun-sign astrology and makes various kinds of magic easier at certain times. Springtime energies assist beneficent works, healing, growth, love and fertility, performed around May Eve, the thirtieth of April; autumnal energies assist works of necromancy, death and darkness performed around All Hallows Eve, the thirty-first of October."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Time, p. 144; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSunspotMaxima a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sunspot maxima as the time to set great changes in motion"@en, "Sonnenfleckenmaxima als Zeit, große Veränderungen anzustoßen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sunspots, intense magnetic vortices appearing in large numbers every eleven years, with a reversal of polarity giving a full cycle of twenty-two years, are said to have unpredictable effects on earth, their maxima being more often than not associated with upheaval and disaster in human affairs. Magically a sunspot maximum is the time to set great changes in motion, while events are at their most sensitive and unstable and the slightest push may have decisive consequences. The maxima of 1968 and 1979 are said to have marked optimistic and pessimistic currents, and the author hopes that 1990 will herald better times. Knowledge of these cycles should not restrict magic but suggest times when it can be practised with more than normal efficiency."@en ;
    skos:note "The work marks one claim of its own as unproven: a correlation between the twenty-two Atu of the tarot and the twenty-two-year cycle, with the Fool and Fortune as its nodes, remains to be proved. The hope for 1990 is the only dated expectation in this part of the work."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Time, p. 144; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Time, p. 145; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicChemognosis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Chemognosis: drugs make occult powers more accessible but confer none by themselves"@en, "Chemognosis: Drogen machen okkulte Kräfte zugänglicher, verleihen aber keine aus sich"@de ;
    skos:definition "Chemical agents of natural and manufactured origin are said always to have played a significant role in shamanism and magic; they can make various occult powers more accessible, but none confers magical abilities by itself. Four factors control the outcome of experiments with magical drugs: the physiological effect of the drug, the training and abilities of the user, any innate magical force in the substance, and any outside magical events. By physiological effect magically useful drugs fall into three classes: hallucinogens, which enhance perception; disinhibitory agents such as alcohol and hashish, which ease the frenzied excitement of ecstatic rites; and hypnotic or narcotic substances, which give degrees of trance and unconsciousness; most drugs show all three effects at different levels of use. The training of the user accounts for much of the difference in effect, so that what moves the untrained only to mild euphoria or nausea may allow the adept to enter trance or ecstasy. Chemical agents are said to be useful only in receptive magic, astral travel, divination and invocation, which the adept should after a while reach without them, and to find very little use in active magic such as sigil casting and enchantment."@en ;
    skos:note "Conditions of failure, three: no drug confers magical ability by itself; hallucination, as distinct from superior perception, occurs when the subject has overdosed or fails to direct his perception to any purpose; and chemical agents find very little application in active magic."@en ;
    skos:note "The prefatory note says the author studied many drugs in a controlled scientific way with manifold safeguards; no protocol, number or result of that study is given in the chapter. Quantities and doses stated in the chapter are not carried in this record."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 147; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 148; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 150; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDrugPoisonCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "All drugs are poisons, and the margin between effect and death cannot be assayed"@en, "Alle Drogen sind Gifte, und der Abstand zwischen Wirkung und Tod lässt sich nicht bestimmen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The chapter opens with a note that to use drugs of any kind is to poison the body, that the difference between sufficient dose and overdose is so variable as to show the danger of toxic substances, and that author and publisher advise against their use; it includes the survey only because omitting this historically important aspect would compromise the book. All drugs are said to cause poisoning, coma and death at some level of use. A closing note repeats that the substances named can act as lethal toxins, and that with many natural psychoactives the difference between a fatal and a merely psychoactive dose is impossible to assay."@en ;
    skos:note "The closing note breaks off in the layer read at the foot of p. 150; its end was not read."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicChemognosis ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 147; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 148; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 150; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDirectedPerception a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Directing perception, so that one communes with magical phenomena rather than having a trip"@en, "Die Wahrnehmung lenken, damit man mit magischen Erscheinungen verkehrt, statt einen Rausch zu haben"@de ;
    skos:definition "The directing of perception is called essential if one is to commune with magical phenomena under a drug rather than have a pleasant or nauseating time. It can be learned in non-drug meditation, brought about by the presence of an adept, or caused by magical forces in the drug substance; outside events may also direct it, as when an experienced initiate leads the neophyte into the correct visions or demonstrates a phenomenon to his enhanced perception. An invocation beforehand at least directs perception."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 148; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDirectedPerceptionBeforeChemognosis a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Without directed perception drug visions are meaningless or horrific"@en, "Ohne gelenkte Wahrnehmung sind Drogenvisionen sinnlos oder grauenhaft"@de ;
    skos:definition "Directing perception is essential if one is to commune with magical phenomena under a drug; failure to direct perception is called the cause of all meaningless and horrific drug visions."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicChemognosis ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicDirectedPerception ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Failure to direct perception causes meaningless and horrific drug visions; one merely has a pleasant or nauseating time, and the experience becomes a disordered trip around the imagination." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 147; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 148; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicEcstaticTechniqueWithSubstances a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Every substance needs an added ecstatic technique to give a useful effect"@en, "Jede Substanz braucht eine hinzugefügte ekstatische Technik, um nützlich zu wirken"@de ;
    skos:definition "All the substances named are said to require an additional ecstatic technique to direct the perception if they are to produce a useful effect."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicChemognosis ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicGnosticMethods ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Without it the substance produces no useful effect." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 150; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicInvocationBeforeChemicals a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "An invocation performed before using a refined or purely chemical substance"@en, "Eine Anrufung vor dem Gebrauch einer gereinigten oder rein chemischen Substanz"@de ;
    skos:definition "When using a refined or purely chemical substance it is called wise to perform an invocation beforehand; at the very least it directs one's perception, and it may succeed in placing a magical charge in the substance itself."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 148; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicInvocationBeforeChemicalsYieldsDirectedPerception a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "An invocation beforehand is said at least to direct perception and perhaps to charge the substance"@en, "Eine vorherige Anrufung soll wenigstens die Wahrnehmung lenken und vielleicht die Substanz laden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The invocation before a chemical substance is said at the very least to direct perception, and it may place a magical charge in the substance."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicInvocationBeforeChemicals ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicDirectedPerception ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 148; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicRespectfulCollecting a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Collecting botanical drugs with the utmost care and respect"@en, "Pflanzliche Drogen mit größter Sorgfalt und Achtung sammeln"@de ;
    skos:definition "A drug may contain innate magical force if it is made from a living thing or specially prepared to contain an occult force, and some preparations include non-drug elements with occult properties, such as part of an animal with which the sorcerer seeks communion. For this reason botanical drugs should be collected with the utmost care and respect."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 148; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPlantSpiritKnowledge a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Knowledge yielded by the spirit of a plant species"@en, "Wissen, das der Geist einer Pflanzenart preisgibt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The spirit of a plant species may yield up its secrets to the user: where to find the plant, its nature and properties, curative and otherwise, and knowledge of other creatures and forces related to it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 148; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicRespectfulCollectingYieldsPlantSpiritKnowledge a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "In return for respectful collection the spirit of the species is said to yield its secrets"@en, "Für achtsames Sammeln soll der Geist der Art seine Geheimnisse preisgeben"@de ;
    skos:definition "In return for collection with care and respect, the spirit of the species may yield up its secrets to the user."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicRespectfulCollecting ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPlantSpiritKnowledge ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 148; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicFlyingOintment a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The flying ointment of the witches"@en, "Die Flugsalbe der Hexen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Flying ointments are found at many points of magical history and in many cultures. Their essential ingredients are said to be a grease base with one or more poisonous plants of the nightshade family, sometimes with aconite, smeared on the skin; their use with a broom handle is given as the origin of the myths. The work's own counsel is to use only sparing amounts and to attempt willed astral travel while asleep rather than comatose."@en ;
    skos:note "The species and places of application named in the chapter are reduced here to their family and to the skin; the record carries the claim and the warning, not a preparation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 149; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAstralTravel a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Astral travel: leaving the body and standing at a chosen place"@en, "Astralreise: den Körper verlassen und an einem gewählten Ort stehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Astral travel is named among the psychic powers now within reach of anyone of moderate determination. Under the alkaloids of the flying ointment real astral travel is said to be possible in the drowsiness and unconsciousness in which hallucinations of flying occur; chemical agents serve such receptive magic. The single experience of having left one's body for a time is said to be worth more than any belief, the only useful preparation for death, and reasonably accessible to any determined person. The author reports that after many months against the psychic censor he found his hands in a dream, pulled them away, and part of him stood at once at a pre-arranged place fifteen miles away."@en ;
    skos:note "Signs of success in the author's account: the transit is instantaneous, like bursting through a balloon or popping through a membrane against huge resistance; he may stand and observe the place for a few moments before being whisked back; and the details are always exactly right, without dreamlike distortion. On the one occasion he thought he had missed, he found he had arrived upside down at a scene otherwise correct in every detail: the one reported miss is read back as a hit."@en ;
    skos:note "Condition of failure in the same account: the censor blocked the attempt for many months."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, New Aeon Magic, p. 114; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 149; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 150; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Occult Priestcraft, p. 184; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Anecdotes, p. 202; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Anecdotes, p. 203; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicFlyingOintmentYieldsAstralTravel a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The alkaloids of the flying ointment are said to make real astral travel possible"@en, "Die Alkaloide der Flugsalbe sollen wirkliche Astralreisen ermöglichen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The nightshade alkaloids are said to cause drowsiness and unconsciousness in which hallucinations of flying occur and in which real astral travel is possible, the aconite numbing the body."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicFlyingOintment ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicAstralTravel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 149; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicFlyingOintmentCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The alkaloids of the flying ointment risk fatal poisoning"@en, "Die Alkaloide der Flugsalbe bergen tödliche Vergiftungsgefahr"@de ;
    skos:definition "All the alkaloids of the flying ointment are said to carry a severe risk of fatal poisoning; it is unwise to overdo or to ingest the mixture, and preferable to use very little and attempt willed astral travel while asleep rather than comatose."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicFlyingOintment ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 149; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicLSDVisions a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "LSD: dazzling visions without magical quality, hard to direct"@en, "LSD: blendende Visionen ohne magische Eigenschaft, schwer zu lenken"@de ;
    skos:definition "Synthetics such as LSD are said to possess no intrinsic magical quality but to produce dazzling erratic visions which, though emotionally charged, seem only to reflect the expectations or fears of the user; because of the fleeting and distorted nature of the experience it is notoriously difficult to direct perception to particular visions in it. In its early days it carried a joyous oceanic vibration and now seems to have acquired an aura of paranoia and madness."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: the substance gives visions that reflect the user, not magical perception, and resists direction."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 149; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicNitrousOxideInspiration a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Nitrous oxide: inspirational visions and a taste of formless samadhi that slip away"@en, "Lachgas: inspirierende Visionen und ein Vorgeschmack des formlosen Samadhi, die entgleiten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Nitrous oxide is said to produce startling visions of an intensely inspirational nature, often seeming to tap the very seat of inspiration, and to give an enticing taste of something approaching formless samadhi; it is probably impossible to direct the trance to magical ends, and its insights tend to slip away on awakening."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: the trance cannot be directed to magical ends and its insights are lost on waking."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 149; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicToadstoolLore a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Fly agaric, the toad and the toadstool, and the Black Elixir"@en, "Fliegenpilz, Kröte und Krötenschwamm, und das Schwarze Elixier"@de ;
    skos:definition "Naturally occurring hallucinogens are called a far richer source of magical perception than synthetics. The fly agaric is said to contain a variety of alkaloids including bufotenine, also found in glands behind the eyes of certain toads, which may explain toads in medieval witches' brews and the fly agaric's standing as the archetypal toadstool; no toad, the author adds, has ever been seen sitting on one by choice. In an afterthought he would not counsel anyone to tread too deeply in the mire of alchemy, but holds the Black Elixir of that tradition to have been almost certainly essence of toad."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 149; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 150; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsilocybeCommunion a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The little mushrooms: communicative if approached with respect"@en, "Die kleinen Pilze: mitteilsam, wenn man ihnen mit Achtung begegnet"@de ;
    skos:definition "Psilocybe mushrooms are said to have had no place in folklore outside the Americas until very recently, which the author reads as the sudden proliferation of a virile hallucinogenic mutant within an insignificant species, and he hopes it will not disappear as mysteriously. They are said to produce all the interesting effects of the fly agaric without its unpleasant side effects, and to be highly communicative if approached with respect, showing the seeker aspects of their collective being and glimpses into himself and the universe. In the anecdotes the author eats a handful of fresh autumnal fungi picked by a fellow sorcerer and, about an hour later, sees a glowing segmented winged body which he afterwards connects with gnat maggots swallowed with the mushrooms."@en ;
    skos:note "The vision is entered at pmc:ChaosMagicLordOfTheGnatsNaming."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 150; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Anecdotes, p. 205; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Anecdotes, p. 206; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicLossOfControlCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Use just enough to stimulate the condition, not so much that control is lost"@en, "Nur so viel, dass der Zustand angeregt wird, nicht so viel, dass die Kontrolle verloren geht"@de ;
    skos:definition "With all excitatory and trance-inducing drugs the trick is said to be to use just enough to stimulate the required condition but not so much that one loses control of it. The trance-inducing substances named are narcotics such as opium, tobacco or mandrake and anaesthetics such as ether and chloroform; the excitatory are alcohol, hashish and hallucinogens."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicChemognosis ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 150; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDrugsInCombatCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "In magical combat the use of drugs may prove disastrous"@en, "Im magischen Kampf kann der Gebrauch von Drogen verhängnisvoll sein"@de ;
    skos:definition "Chemical agents find very little use in active magic, and in magical combat their use may prove disastrous."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicMagicalCombat ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Chemognosis, p. 150; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAnyGodsName a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Magic works equally in any god's name and for any motive"@en, "Magie wirkt gleich in jedes Gottes Namen und aus jedem Beweggrund"@de ;
    skos:definition "Physical processes alone will never wholly explain the universe, life and consciousness, and religious answers are called wishful thinking over a bottomless pit of ignorance, so magicians must develop models beyond both. To the magician it is self-evident that there is some level of reality other than the physical. Medieval magicians thought their powers came from God or the Devil; in fact, the work says, magic works equally well in any god's name for good, evil, neutral or indifferent motives, and there is no need beyond the psychological to anthropomorphise the other reality. Where science declares consciousness illusory because it fits no mechanistic scheme, magicians extend it in some degree to all things, trees, amulets and planets included."@en ;
    skos:note "The contrast with psychology, which explains different responses by the ego, and with the materialist assumption of free will, is answered at pmc:ChaosMagicKiaPsychonaut. No opposing work is named."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, p. 151; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSecretOfTheUniverse a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Chaos, the secret of the universe: consciousness, magic and chaos as one spontaneity"@en, "Das Chaos, das Geheimnis des Universums: Bewusstsein, Magie und Chaos als eine Spontaneität"@de ;
    skos:definition "Consciousness, magic and chaos may be the same thing. Consciousness makes things happen spontaneously without prior cause, usually in the brain, where will tickles the nerves, occasionally outside the body when it performs magic; any act of will is magic, and so is any act of conscious perception. All events, the origin of the universe included, are said to happen basically by magic, arising without a final prior cause; physical laws are statistical approximations, like a million sixes from six million dice, and have themselves just arisen and may cease. This root of phenomena is called Chaos; it cannot be understood, since our understanding is built of matter that mainly obeys statistical causality; the universe is the thoughts of Chaos, which we may understand while not the Chaos they arise from. Science asks how, religion why, art which, and magic what, and answers that consciousness and the universe are spontaneous, magical and chaotic. The force at their centre is whimsical, neither spiritual nor moral; nothing is true, some information is relatively useful, and the universe is a practical joke that leaves us free. In the new paradigm Chaos is the inexpressible pregnant void from which existence arises, nearly imperceptible, best described by change or as the one point at rest, too infinite to be much use as a god."@en ;
    skos:note "The section closes with a litany of credo sentences, among them that change is the only constant, that apparent order increases the possibilities of chaos, that the speaker requires no justification, and that he will believe whatever brings him joy, power and ecstasy; it is prefaced as perhaps bad lyric poetry."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, Chaos: The Secret of the Universe, p. 153; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, Chaos: The Secret of the Universe, p. 154; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, Baphomet, p. 157; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, Baphomet, p. 158; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicFiveAeons a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The aeons: shamanic, pagan, monotheistic, atheistic and chaoist"@en, "Die Äonen: schamanisch, heidnisch, monotheistisch, atheistisch und chaoistisch"@de ;
    skos:definition "The work orders history in aeons marked by the passage of great metaphysical paradigms rather than by set periods. In the first, shamanic aeon men recognised the animating spirit of living beings as the Horned God. Agriculture and settled life brought the pagan aeon, in which the force was fragmented into polytheistic and pantheistic deities and magic went underground. In the monotheistic aeon religion became an instrument of the state, with gods defined as idealised male humans, and magic was suppressed because the priests were not adept at it; the Horned God reappeared as the Devil. In the atheistic aeon, through which the leading cultures are now passing, god became man, stripped of psychic capacity and given technology, and the life force became the hidden god. On the threshold of the chaoist aeon a new conception of psychic reality is forming, the leading edge of quantum physics seeming to give a theoretical basis to phenomena rediscovered by parapsychology and ancient magic. The aeonic litany of the Mass gives a parallel sequence: Great Spirit, Horned God, the Devil, the Hidden One, Baphomet."@en ;
    skos:note "The monotheistic God is called an idealised image of ourselves, our fathers or our kings writ large, made childishly small by the scale the telescope revealed; Yahweh, Jehovah, Allah and Buddha are named as such idealisations. No text of those religions is cited; entered here and not as a dispute."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, Baphomet, p. 156; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, Baphomet, p. 157; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, The Mass of Chaos, p. 131; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, The Mass of Chaos, p. 132; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Paradigms, p. 191; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicHornedGodNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "The Horned God, the animating spirit recognised in the shamanic aeon"@en, "Der Gehörnte Gott, der im schamanischen Äon erkannte belebende Geist"@de ;
    skos:definition "In the first, shamanic aeon men recognised the animating spirit of living beings, often depicted as a man with antlers: a force without morality that could not be bargained with or placated, horned because it gave powers over animals and a horned man because it was something extra men could acquire, its double horns marking a force both good and evil, light and dark, beautiful and terrible. In the monotheistic aeon it reappeared as the anti-god of those systems, and its devotees met secretly as witches and sorcerers. Shamanic spirit congress usually includes a horned god even where there are no horned animals, and Ialdaboath is called another manifestation of it."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Horned God" ;
    pm:nameRole "god; the animating spirit of living beings in the first aeon" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, Baphomet, p. 156; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, Baphomet, p. 157; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Shamanism, p. 171; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Gnosticism, p. 175; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTribalPsychicEdge a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "A psychic edge for oneself and one's tribe through appreciation of the life force"@en, "Ein seelischer Vorsprung für sich und den eigenen Stamm durch das Erfassen der Lebenskraft"@de ;
    skos:definition "By careful observation, meditation and training it was possible, the work says, to give oneself and one's tribe a psychic edge in a hostile environment through appreciation of the force symbolised by the Horned God; these early psychic abilities, with a high intelligence, rapidly made humans the planet's most successful species."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, Baphomet, p. 156; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicBaphometNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Baphomet, the psychic field of all living beings on the planet, the magicians' god"@en, "Baphomet, das seelische Feld aller Lebewesen des Planeten, der Gott der Magier"@de ;
    skos:definition "Baphomet is the psychic field generated by the totality of living beings on this planet, the spirit of its life energy, which in modern times has reasserted itself under this symbol where the ancient shamans used the Horned God. It has been represented as Pan, Pangenitor, Pamphage, All-Begetter and All-Destroyer, as Shiva-Kali, as Abraxas, as the animal-headed Devil of sex and death, as the evil Archon set over this world, as Ishtar or Astaroth, as the Anima Mundi, or simply as Goddess, and also as the Eagle, Baron Samedi, Thanateros or Cernunnos. The name is said probably to come from a Greek phrase meaning union with wisdom. It is conventionally shown as a hermaphrodite horned human with mammalian and reptilian features, and should also carry protozoan, insect, floral and necrotic elements, with a torch between the horns, for spirit is most easily visualised as light; its sexual aspects are always emphasised. In the Mass it is the terrestrial life-current, a horned theriomorphic deity of androgynous, winged, reptilian, mammalian and human aspect; the Kia is reabsorbed into it at death; in the paradigm of morphic fields it is the vast composite egregore to which all life on earth contributes, also called the Great Mother and the Great Archon."@en ;
    skos:note "Life and death are called a single phenomenon through which the life force continually reincarnates; a denial of death is a denial of life, and religions that deny death are called basically anti-life. No religion is named."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Baphomet" ;
    pm:nameRole "god; the psychic field of the life on this planet" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, The Mass of Chaos, p. 131; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, The Rites of Chaos, Extreme Unction: The Final Enchantment, p. 140; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, Baphomet, p. 158; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, Baphomet, p. 159; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, Baphomet, p. 161; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Paradigms, p. 194; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicCommunionWithBaphomet a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Invoking the god of the life force with love play, wine and drugs, then concentration"@en, "Den Gott der Lebenskraft mit Liebesspiel, Wein und Drogen anrufen, dann mit Konzentration"@de ;
    skos:definition "The spirit of the life force is the spirit of the dual ecstasy, procreation and reabsorption, sex and death. It is to be invoked with wild uninhibited love play and with wine and strange drugs that thrill and exalt the vitality and imagination; lastly the magician draws his exhilarated consciousness into communion with the god by profound concentration and visualisation. The section ends with an appeal to the god to come forth in horned majesty as the power of the air and grant the windsight and the windspeech."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, Baphomet, p. 159; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicLifeForceToWield a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The magic life force to wield for good or ill, and the windsight and windspeech"@en, "Die magische Lebenskraft, für Gutes oder Übles zu führen, und Windsicht und Windsprache"@de ;
    skos:definition "Communion with the god of the life force is said to put the magic life force in the magician's hands to wield for good or ill; the powers petitioned of it as the power of the air are the windsight and the windspeech, which the work does not define further."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, Baphomet, p. 159; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicCommunionWithBaphometYieldsLifeForce a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Communion with the god of the life force is said to make the life force the magician's to wield"@en, "Die Gemeinschaft mit dem Gott der Lebenskraft soll die Lebenskraft dem Magier zur Verfügung stellen"@de ;
    skos:definition "After the invocation with love play, wine and drugs, profound concentration and visualisation draw the exhilarated consciousness into communion with the god, and the magic life force is said then to be the magician's to wield."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicCommunionWithBaphomet ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicLifeForceToWield ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, Baphomet, p. 159; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicReptilianAtavisms a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The dragon in the skull: myth's reptiles as atavisms of the oldest brain"@en, "Der Drache im Schädel: die Reptilien des Mythos als Atavismen des ältesten Gehirns"@de ;
    skos:definition "Most mythologies keep lore of primal reptilian energies older than the gods, the Leviathan-like serpents encircling the universe or chaotic dragons from which existence springs, slain, imprisoned or suppressed by the gods, and most demons have reptilian features. The work rejects the explanations by phallic resemblance, by the snake's seeming self-renewal, or by ancestral memory of dangerous reptiles, since dinosaurs were long extinct and our ancestors fought mammals. The dragon of mythology sleeps inside our heads: evolution has left a man, a wolf and a crocodile in the skull, adding new parts without restructuring, and all dragons, serpents and scaly demons are reptile atavisms arising from the older parts; in myth the gods, as human consciousness, suppress the titans and dragons of the older consciousness. Society and religion keep the dragon and wolf suppressed except for war; for the magician these atavistic forces are a source of personal power, which is why Baphomet is shown in composite man, mammal and serpent form."@en ;
    skos:note "The rejected explanations are attributed to no one; entered here and not as a dispute."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, Baphomet, p. 159; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, Baphomet, p. 160; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, Baphomet, p. 161; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicRaisingTheSerpentPower a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Arousing the older brains: aetheric force raised into the cranium, beast-form visualisation, sigils"@en, "Die älteren Gehirne wecken: ätherische Kraft in den Schädel heben, Tiergestalt visualisieren, Sigillen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The traditions of magic preserve techniques for arousing the sleeping dragons and wolves of the older brains. If the body's aetheric forces are directed upward into the cranium, the reptilian circuits are the first to be activated, which Oriental esotericism calls raising the Kundalini. The older centres can also be activated in intense excitement or meditative quiescence, and gnosis can be directed to these levels by visualising oneself in the required beast form and by using sigils to reach the subconscious behaviour programmes."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, Baphomet, p. 160; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, Baphomet, p. 161; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDragonMind a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The dragon mind: creating nasty demons and projecting enchantments of like nature"@en, "Der Drachengeist: üble Dämonen erschaffen und Bezauberungen gleicher Art senden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The aroused dragon mind is said to find magical application in the creation of powerful and rather nasty demons and in the projection of enchantments of a similar nature. Its programmes extend neither to compassion nor conscience and contain only enough forethought for hunting, killing, eating and reproducing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, Baphomet, p. 161; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicRaisingTheSerpentPowerYieldsDragonMind a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Raising the aetheric force into the cranium is said to activate the reptilian circuits first"@en, "Die ätherische Kraft in den Schädel zu heben soll zuerst die Reptilienschaltkreise wecken"@de ;
    skos:definition "Directed upward into the cranium, the body's aetheric forces are said first to activate the reptilian circuits; gnosis directed by beast-form visualisation and sigils reaches the same levels, and the dragon mind so aroused serves the creation of demons and enchantments of its own nature."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicRaisingTheSerpentPower ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicDragonMind ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, Baphomet, p. 160; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, Baphomet, p. 161; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicKundaliniLingeringCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The Kundalini must not linger in the reptilian centres"@en, "Die Kundalini darf nicht in den Reptilienzentren verweilen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Mindful of the dangers of the technique, the Oriental magicians are said to have insisted that the Kundalini must not be allowed to linger in the reptilian circuits but must be made to enter the higher cerebral centres."@en ;
    skos:note "The work does not say what the danger is."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicRaisingTheSerpentPower ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, Baphomet, p. 161; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsychicCensorPsychonaut a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The psychic censor, which keeps magic from being noticed"@en, "Der psychische Zensor, der verhindert, dass Magie bemerkt wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "The physical part of ourselves is said to abhor chaos and magic, and a very powerful censor mechanism prevents us using or noticing all but a small fraction of it. People presented with real magical events somehow manage not to notice. The censor shields us from intrusions from other realities, edits out most telepathic communication, blinds us to prescience, and reduces our ability to register significant coincidences or recall dreams; ordinary physical life would be impossible without it, like living permanently under hallucinogens. It is a material thing protecting the mind from the awesome strangeness of the psychic dimension, which appears as chaos. It is most active on the awareness level, less on the robotic, least on the gnostic, where much of the mind is silenced; on the dream level it often prevents the command to act from penetrating or the memory from reaching awareness. It is less active in trance but often blocks awareness of magical events from reaching other levels. In the author's account it goes to almost any lengths to prevent one experiencing or remembering otherworld phenomena, and anyone who strives against his own resistance to these phenomena will achieve results."@en ;
    skos:note "The censor stands in the work as the general account of why magic goes unnoticed and why attempts fail; every unobserved effect is open to being assigned to it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Group Magical Experiments, p. 119; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, The Psychic Censor, p. 162; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, The Psychic Censor, p. 163; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Anecdotes, p. 202; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Anecdotes, p. 206; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicConfrontingMagicCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Forced to notice something magical, people may become terrified, nauseated and ill"@en, "Gezwungen, etwas Magisches zu bemerken, können Menschen in Schrecken geraten, übel werden und erkranken"@de ;
    skos:definition "If people are forced to notice something incontrovertibly magical they may become terrified, nauseated and ill."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicPsychicCensorPsychonaut ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, The Psychic Censor, p. 162; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicBypassingTheCensor a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Bypassing the censor: an almost maddening volume of telepathy, precognition and coincidence"@en, "Den Zensor umgehen: eine fast wahnsinnig machende Fülle von Telepathie, Vorausschau und Zufall"@de ;
    skos:definition "Magicians have tricks for selectively bypassing the censor, and many techniques draw consciousness away from the awareness level. On the robotic level, in absent-minded preoccupation, strange flashes of almost subliminal perception occur, which the censor often keeps from entering awareness; if this barrier is overcome, an almost maddening volume of telepathy, short-term precognition and improbable coincidence can be perceived. Most effective magical systems have developed one or more methods of entering the gnostic level deliberately, the least protected. The author reports many hundreds of unrecorded instances of telepathy, precognition, coincidence and spell casting."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, The Psychic Censor, p. 162; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, The Psychic Censor, p. 163; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Anecdotes, p. 206; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicGnosticMethodsYieldBypassingTheCensor a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Entering gnosis deliberately is said to pass the censor where it is weakest"@en, "Absichtlich in die Gnosis einzutreten soll den Zensor dort umgehen, wo er am schwächsten ist"@de ;
    skos:definition "The gnostic level of quiescent concentration or ecstatic excitement is least protected by the censor because much of the mind is silenced there, and so the effective magical systems have methods of entering it deliberately."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicGnosticMethods ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicBypassingTheCensor ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, The Psychic Censor, p. 163; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicNoHigherSelf a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "No higher self and no true will: nobody is at home"@en, "Kein höheres Selbst und kein wahrer Wille: niemand ist zu Hause"@de ;
    skos:definition "The notion of a higher self or true will is called a curious error misappropriated by occult systems from the monotheistic religions. No part of one's beliefs about oneself cannot be modified by sufficiently powerful psychological techniques; the proper stimuli can turn communists into fascists, saints into devils, the meek into heroes and back, and with drugs, brainwashing and extreme persuasion a man can be made the devotee of another ideology, country or religion. There is no sovereign sanctuary within that represents our real nature, nobody at home in the internal fortress; the ego is cobbled together from the accident of birth and experience, the mind is an extension of the body, and the only part of ourselves above that mutable structure is the Kia."@en ;
    skos:note "The systems that hold a higher self or true will are not named; entered here and not as a dispute."@en ;
    skos:note "The power of extreme persuasion is stated as a fact about minds, not offered as a practice of the magician; it is not entered as a separate capacity."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, The Demon Choronzon, p. 164; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicEgo a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The ego as a castle against reality that is also a prison"@en, "Das Ich als Burg gegen die Wirklichkeit, die zugleich ein Gefängnis ist"@de ;
    skos:definition "The ego is what the mind thinks we are, an image grown from body, sex, race, religion, culture, education, socialisation, fears and desires, under pressure to be integrated, assertive and defended. The more strongly we identify with something the more strongly we must reject its opposite, so the strongest, most obsessive egos belong to the least complete beings. Developing an ego is like building a castle against reality: it gives some defence and purpose, but the larger it is the more it invites attack, it must ultimately crumble, and all fortresses are also prisons, since beliefs imply the rejection of their opposites. Belief in a god and belief in one's ego are called the same thing."@en ;
    skos:note "Mystics and religiously minded magicians who describe their experience as transcendence and the merging of the ego in godhead are said to have done nothing of the sort but to have inflated their ego by gnostic exaltation into a cultivated immense god, the same process as the black magician's, with a show of humility; no mystic or text is named."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, The Demon Choronzon, p. 165; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, The Demon Choronzon, p. 166; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicExaltingAPrincipleCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "To exalt any principle eventually attracts its opposite"@en, "Ein Prinzip zu erhöhen zieht am Ende sein Gegenteil an"@de ;
    skos:definition "For the strongly identified ego there is the further problem that to exalt any principle will eventually attract its opposite: those who exalt strength will be drawn into weakness, those who strive for good will become embroiled in evil."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicEgo ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, The Demon Choronzon, p. 165; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicHolyGuardianAngelCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "What the invoked Holy Guardian Angel communicates comes from one's own mind"@en, "Was der angerufene Heilige Schutzengel mitteilt, stammt aus dem eigenen Geist"@de ;
    skos:definition "The inflation of the ego happens in exactly the same way when a magician invokes his Holy Guardian Angel. Any names, images, symbols and directives he receives are only exaggerated artefacts of his own mind and ego and possibly telepathic fragments from other people; because he obtains them in a gnostic state he is likely to accept them uncritically, and since gnosis also unleashes subconscious creativity the messages are the more alluring if strung together with unexpected cleverness."@en ;
    skos:note "Two conditions of failure follow: whoever invokes the Holy Guardian Angel expecting signs and manifestations will usually be given them by his own genius and magical capacities if enough gnosis is employed, and whoever enters an exalted state unplanned will have the free belief it generates attach to any incipient mystical idea; in both cases the real thing is missed. The work thus declares that signs announced in advance are supplied by the expectation."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicInvokingKia ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, The Demon Choronzon, p. 165; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, The Demon Choronzon, p. 166; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicChoronzonNamingPsychonaut a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Choronzon, the demons of obsession with the products of one's own genius"@en, "Choronzon, die Dämonen der Besessenheit von den Erzeugnissen des eigenen Genius"@de ;
    skos:definition "We each have a real Holy Guardian Angel, the Kia, our power of consciousness, magic and genius, and a regrettable capacity to become obsessed with the mere products of our genius, mistaking them for the genius itself. These obsessional side effects are given a generic name, Choronzon, or perhaps the demons Choronzon, since its name is multiple. Choronzon is to be banished whenever it manifests."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Choronzon" ;
    pm:nameRole "demon, or many demons; obsession with the products of one's own genius" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, The Demon Choronzon, p. 166; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, The Demon Choronzon, p. 167; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicChoronzonCautelaPsychonaut a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "To worship the products of one's genius imprisons one in madness"@en, "Die Erzeugnisse des eigenen Genius anzubeten sperrt einen in den Wahnsinn"@de ;
    skos:definition "To worship the creations named Choronzon is to imprison oneself in madness and to invoke eventual disaster; both the religious maniac and the black magician gain charisma and mission from their obsessions, but their quest is futile, since they cannot get beyond their own inflated fears and desires to the formless power source within."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicChoronzonNamingPsychonaut ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, The Demon Choronzon, p. 166; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicInvokingKia a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Invoking the real Holy Guardian Angel: union with the rejected, and many diverse acts of genius"@en, "Den wirklichen Heiligen Schutzengel anrufen: Vereinigung mit dem Verworfenen und viele verschiedene Taten des Genius"@de ;
    skos:definition "To invoke the real Holy Guardian Angel or Kia is called paradoxically difficult: it has no form to grip in imagination and cannot be willed or perceived, being itself the root of will and perception. There are perhaps only two things one can do. First, the ego can be put in its place by deliberately seeking union with anything one has rejected. Second, Kia can be felt as the root of all acts of consciousness, magic and genius by performing as diverse and extensive a series of such acts as possible. Invoke often, as the oracle said, and banish Choronzon whenever it manifests."@en ;
    skos:note "The oracle is not named."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, The Demon Choronzon, p. 166; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, The Demon Choronzon, p. 167; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicInvokingKiaYieldsKia a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Many diverse acts of consciousness, magic and genius are said to let Kia be felt as their root"@en, "Viele verschiedene Taten des Bewusstseins, der Magie und des Genius sollen Kia als ihre Wurzel fühlbar machen"@de ;
    skos:definition "By performing as diverse and extensive a series of acts of consciousness, magic and genius as possible, the hidden god-force Kia is said to be felt as the root of them all."@en ;
    skos:note "The capacity is the feeling of Kia as the root of such acts, not Kia itself; the work grants no sign by which it could be confirmed, since any expected sign would be supplied by the invoker's own genius (pmc:ChaosMagicHolyGuardianAngelCautela)."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicInvokingKia ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicKiaPsychonaut ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, The Demon Choronzon, p. 167; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicShamanism a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Shamanism, the oldest tradition and fountainhead of all occult systems"@en, "Schamanismus, die älteste Überlieferung und Quelle aller okkulten Systeme"@de ;
    skos:definition "Shamanism is called the oldest magical and mystical tradition, from which all religious arts and magical sciences originate; it is still practised on the southern continents, mainly in hunting societies, has been almost destroyed elsewhere, and survives in part in European witchcraft. Despite enormous geographical separation shamanic cultures are said to share almost identical methods, and it is shamanic knowledge and power that contemporary magicians seek to rediscover. Shamanic power cannot be progressively accumulated like other technology; it is so hard to master that a tradition needs a continual influx of talent to avoid degenerating, which is why shamans describe decline from past glories, and only an exceptional practitioner wins back the legendary powers. Central is an otherworld, an astral or aetheric dimension of powers, entities and forces through which real effects are created in this world, entered through perilous clashing gates comparable to the Abyss, by trance and by dream. Spirit congress includes nature spirits, animal and plant entities and servitors, shades of the dead, incubi and succubi, and usually a horned god. Prospective shamans are usually chosen from those of nervous disposition."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Shamanism, p. 169; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Shamanism, p. 170; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Shamanism, p. 171; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicShamanicJourney a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The shaman's soul journey through the otherworld in trance"@en, "Die Seelenreise des Schamanen durch die andere Welt in Trance"@de ;
    skos:definition "The shaman's soul journeys through the otherworld while in an ecstatic or drug-induced state of trance. Exorcism and curing, the main skills shared with the community, are undertaken in trance and ecstasy during which an otherworld journey is made to seek a cure; magical attack and protection are performed for clients, and shamans fight each other for supremacy, often in their otherworld animal shapes."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Shamanism, p. 170; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicOtherworldJourneyPowers a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "What the otherworld journey is undertaken for: divinatory knowledge, cures, blows to enemies, game"@en, "Wofür die Reise in die andere Welt unternommen wird: Wahrsagewissen, Heilung, Schläge gegen Feinde, Wild"@de ;
    skos:definition "The journey through the otherworld may be undertaken for divinatory knowledge, to cure sickness, to deliver a blow to enemies, or to find game animals; real effects in this world are said to be created through that dimension."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Shamanism, p. 170; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicShamanicJourneyYieldsOtherworldPowers a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The soul journey is said to bring knowledge, cures, blows to enemies and game"@en, "Die Seelenreise soll Wissen, Heilung, Schläge gegen Feinde und Wild bringen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Undertaken in ecstatic or drug-induced trance, the journey through the otherworld is said to obtain divinatory knowledge, cure sickness, strike enemies or find game."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicShamanicJourney ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicOtherworldJourneyPowers ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Shamanism, p. 170; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicShamanicInitiation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Shamanic initiation: otherworld journey, spirits, dismemberment and reassembly"@en, "Schamanische Initiation: Reise in die andere Welt, Geister, Zerstückelung und Wiederzusammensetzung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Initiation invokes a journey into the otherworld, a meeting with spirits and a death-rebirth experience, in which the candidate has a vision of his body being dismembered, often by fantastic beings or animal spirits, and reassembled from the wreckage."@en ;
    skos:note "Sign of success: the new body invariably contains an extra part, an additional bone, magical quartz stones or an animal spirit."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Shamanism, p. 170; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAethericForceField a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The aetheric force field in the body, exuded through the navel, the eyes or the hands"@en, "Das ätherische Kraftfeld im Körper, durch Nabel, Augen oder Hände ausgesandt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The extra part found in the reassembled body of the shamanic initiate is said graphically to symbolise the location of the aetheric force field within the body or the addition of extra powers to it. In most shamanic systems this aetheric force is exuded through the navel region for short-range magic, or through the eyes or hands, and it is the same as the Chi, Ki, Kundalini or aura. The author reports that after some months of practising Spare's death posture he entered a strange ecstatic state, exhilarating beyond expectation, in which he hovered as a point of awareness outside his body and could examine the ball of ectoplasmic force each of us contains in the midsection."@en ;
    skos:note "Sign of success in the author's account of the death posture: the sudden ecstatic state that seemed a gateway to magical experience. The death posture itself is described in Liber Null and not in this part."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Shamanism, p. 170; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Shamanism, p. 171; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Anecdotes, p. 204; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicShamanicInitiationYieldsAethericForceField a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The death-rebirth vision is said to locate the aetheric force field or add powers to it"@en, "Die Vision von Tod und Wiedergeburt soll das ätherische Kraftfeld verorten oder ihm Kräfte hinzufügen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The vision of dismemberment and reassembly with an extra part is said to symbolise the location of the aetheric force field within the body or the addition of extra powers to it."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicShamanicInitiation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicAethericForceField ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Shamanism, p. 170; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPhysiologicalControl a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Physiological control against heat, cold and pain, and firewalking"@en, "Körperbeherrschung gegen Hitze, Kälte und Schmerz, und das Feuerlaufen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Some shamans are said to cultivate enormous physiological control with which to resist extremes of heat, cold and pain; firewalking, in which fierce heat is magically prevented from scorching flesh, is called a very common feature of the tradition, found worldwide. The new aeon chapter mentions the odd shaman who still insists on walking over pits of fire."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, New Aeon Magic, p. 114; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Shamanism, p. 170; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Shamanism, p. 171; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicShamanicTools a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The shaman's drum or rattle, and power objects"@en, "Trommel oder Rassel des Schamanen, und Kraftgegenstände"@de ;
    skos:definition "Shamanic tools vary widely but usually include a noise-making device, a drum or a snake-bone rattle, to call spirits and induce trance, and power objects, most commonly quartz crystals."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Shamanism, p. 171; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicShamanicTrance a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Trance in which spirits are called"@en, "Trance, in der Geister gerufen werden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The trance of the shaman, induced by drum or rattle, in which spirits are called and the otherworld is entered."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Shamanism, p. 170; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Shamanism, p. 171; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicShamanicToolsYieldShamanicTrance a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Drum and rattle are said to call spirits and induce trance"@en, "Trommel und Rassel sollen Geister rufen und Trance herbeiführen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The noise-making device of the shaman is said to call spirits and induce trance."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicShamanicTools ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicShamanicTrance ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Shamanism, p. 171; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicGnosticism a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Gnosticism as the completed synthesis of the themes of magic"@en, "Gnostizismus als vollendete Synthese der Themen der Magie"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Gnostic cults of the first and second centuries, notably in Alexandria, are said to have been both antique and highly advanced, violently suppressed by hierarchical Christianity, though Jesus is not to blame for the religion practised in his name; they survived underground, the Cathars and Albigensians possessed some of their knowledge, and they synthesised Greek, Egyptian and Oriental learning that resurfaced in medieval and renaissance magic, the Templars, witchcraft and Rosicrucianism. Gnosticism was concerned above all with Gnosis, mystical experience, as against Pistis, faith. The Supreme Being was wholly ineffable, like the Tao or the Void, with a fragment of it in every living being, and Gnosis meant experiencing that spark within. Having experienced it, the Gnostics knew they could not be touched and were free to do anything, choosing libertinism or asceticism, usually the opposite of custom; matter was evil because impermanent, and only the spark was immortal, reincarnating until union with the infinite. It was never an organised religion but a series of elitist cults led by figures such as Simon Magus, Valentinus and Apollonius of Tyana, each teacher adapting his gnosis to local belief. Their magic included familiar spirits, necromancy and potions for erotic and dream-inducing purposes, but their main practices were orgiastic, telesmatic and incantatory; their engraved gems would have functioned as talismans and amulets, their votive statuary as fetishistic centrepieces, and many words of power and barbarous names of later magic, as in the Bornless or Headless ritual, and Abracadabra from Abraxas, come from their incantations. The timeless themes of magic appear complete in Gnosticism because it drew on Egyptian learning, the Greek mysteries and the East, each preserving traditions from shamanism."@en ;
    skos:note "The work reports Gnostic counter-scriptures in which Yahweh is a vicious senile fool persecuting humanity and the serpent who gave knowledge humanity's friend, and Jesus a true messenger whose crucifixion was meaningless. It also says that if one were trying to rediscover or invent the Necronomicon of the Lovecraft mythos, Gnosticism would be the best source. No Gnostic text is cited by title."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Gnosticism, p. 173; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Gnosticism, p. 174; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Gnosticism, p. 175; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Gnosticism, p. 176; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Gnosticism, p. 177; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAeonsAndArchons a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The Gnostic intermediate world of Aeons and Archons"@en, "Die gnostische Zwischenwelt der Äonen und Archonten"@de ;
    skos:definition "How the infinite fragmented itself and descended into matter was debated endlessly among the Gnostics, whose theories were sexual allegories, commentaries on psychology, ridicule of other religions, or deliberate ridicule of understanding the process at all. In one form the ultimate reality gives rise to Aeons, usually thirty, spiritual principalities surrounding the material universe, an idea the work sees reappearing as the thirty Aethers of John Dee's visions; tensions among them form Archons, or rulers. In other systems the ultimate is itself the First Archon, from which subsequent Archons, usually seven, the Hebdomad, evolved by Ennoia, thought projection; the Ennoia of the first Archon produced Barbelo. From these principles arose the creator of this world, sometimes sevenfold and identified with the planets, responsible for the creation of material beings into which the ultimate breathed a vital spark."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Gnosticism, p. 174; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Gnosticism, p. 175; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicBarbeloNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Barbelo, known to us as Babalon or Nuit, in whom reabsorption is sought"@en, "Barbelo, uns als Babalon oder Nuit bekannt, in der die Wiederaufnahme gesucht wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "Barbelo or Barbelon is the being of female or androgynous nature produced by the Ennoia of the first Archon, or identified with the Great Silence in which the first Archon manifested. The work identifies her with Babalon or Nuit, the great star mother in whom one must seek reabsorption to penetrate the highest mystery; the Gnostic sexual rites gave a foretaste of the final reabsorption into Babalon."@en ;
    skos:note "The identification with Babalon and Nuit is the work's own, not a Gnostic one."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Barbelo" ;
    pm:nameRole "female or androgynous emanation; the star mother of reabsorption" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Gnosticism, p. 174; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Gnosticism, p. 175; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Gnosticism, p. 176; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicIaldaboathNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Ialdaboath, the animal-headed creator of this world, another form of the horned god"@en, "Ialdaboath, der tierköpfige Schöpfer dieser Welt, eine weitere Gestalt des gehörnten Gottes"@de ;
    skos:definition "The force responsible for the creation of this world is variously called Ialdaboath, Sabaoth, Iao and many other names, sometimes sevenfold and identified with the planets, androgynous or male with an animal-headed manifestation, and held responsible for creating material beings. The work calls Ialdaboath yet another manifestation of the ubiquitous horned god, known to the Templars as Baphomet and to the Christians as the Devil; in some Gnostic systems the dragon force appears as the world-serpent or Leviathan encircling the universe and biting its tail. Gnostic sexual rites were designed to cheat the evil Archon of further human victims."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Ialdaboath" ;
    pm:nameRole "creator of the material world; the evil Archon" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Gnosticism, p. 175; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Gnosticism, p. 176; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicGnosticSexualSacrament a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The Gnostic orgiastic sacrament of mixed sexual elixirs"@en, "Das gnostische orgiastische Sakrament der vermischten sexuellen Elixiere"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Gnostics' orgiastic rites are said to have included the consumption as sacraments of the mixed male and female sexual elixirs and menstrual blood after coitus. Most sects considered reproduction a repetition of a fundamental error, and their sexual rites were designed to cheat the evil Archon of further human victims."@en ;
    skos:note "A further practice the work reports only as a reputation of the Gnostics is not carried here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Gnosticism, p. 176; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicForetasteOfReabsorption a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "An inspirational foretaste of the final reabsorption into Babalon"@en, "Ein inspirierender Vorgeschmack der endgültigen Wiederaufnahme in Babalon"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Gnostic sexual rites are said to have given an inspirational foretaste of the final and ultimate reabsorption into Babalon."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Gnosticism, p. 176; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicGnosticSexualSacramentYieldsForetaste a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The Gnostic sexual rites are said to give a foretaste of reabsorption into Babalon"@en, "Die gnostischen Sexualriten sollen einen Vorgeschmack der Wiederaufnahme in Babalon geben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Besides cheating the evil Archon of new victims, the sexual rites are said to have been designed to give an inspirational foretaste of the final reabsorption into Babalon."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicGnosticSexualSacrament ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicForetasteOfReabsorption ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Gnosticism, p. 176; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicFiveHumanNeeds a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Five human needs that magical and religious enterprises serve"@en, "Fünf menschliche Bedürfnisse, denen magische und religiöse Unternehmungen dienen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Magical, mystical and religious enterprises seek to fulfil five basic needs: techniques of emotional engineering; a sense of meaning; some means of intercession or intervention; an explanation of death; and a social structure or cult. The needs are interrelated, and solving some makes others less urgent; an occult priest should be capable of dealing with all of them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Occult Priestcraft, p. 179; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicEmotionalEngineering a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Emotional engineering: frenzied ecstasy, absolute quiescence, and the laughter of the gods"@en, "Gefühlstechnik: rasende Ekstase, vollkommene Stille und das Lachen der Götter"@de ;
    skos:definition "Emotional engineering covers all practices designed to stimulate or control emotional states. If the magician wishes to put himself into or out of any emotional state he should have the techniques to do so; the process needs no justification, his willing it is enough, and since emotion cannot be escaped in a human incarnation it is better to be its master than its slave. The main methods are the gnostic ones: casting oneself into a frenzied ecstasy, stilling the mind to a point of absolute quiescence, and evoking the laughter of the gods by combining laughter with the contemplation of paradox. The occult priest should be able to instruct anyone in them."@en ;
    skos:note "The work holds that entertainment, not drugs, is the greatest threat to religion, having usurped many functions of the priest, honestly but still manipulatively."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Occult Priestcraft, p. 179; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Occult Priestcraft, p. 180; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMasteryOverEmotion a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Power over oneself: nothing touches one unless one wills it"@en, "Macht über sich selbst: nichts berührt einen, es sei denn, man will es"@de ;
    skos:definition "Anyone who fully masters the techniques of emotional engineering is said to have achieved a tremendous power over himself, more valuable than health, love, fame or riches: he has set himself free from the effects of the world, and nothing can touch him unless he wills it; as has been said, the sage who knows how can live comfortably in hell."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Occult Priestcraft, p. 180; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicEmotionalEngineeringYieldsMasteryOverEmotion a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Mastering emotional engineering is said to free one from the effects of the world"@en, "Die Gefühlstechnik zu meistern soll von den Wirkungen der Welt befreien"@de ;
    skos:definition "Full mastery of frenzied ecstasy, absolute quiescence and the laughter of the gods is said to give power over oneself, so that nothing touches one unless one wills it."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicEmotionalEngineering ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicMasteryOverEmotion ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Occult Priestcraft, p. 180; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMeaningAsMotivation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Meaning is motivation, arises from dualities, and is destroyed by absolute answers"@en, "Sinn ist Antrieb, entsteht aus Gegensätzen und wird durch unbedingte Antworten zerstört"@de ;
    skos:definition "Meaning is motivation: anything that gives rise to behaviour provides meaning, from the body's pain, pleasure, hunger and sexuality through the mind's desire for knowledge, power and gratification to motives called spiritual and the meaning of meaning itself. Meaning arises from differentiating experience into pain and pleasure, good and evil, interesting and uninteresting; we can only perceive difference, so meaning depends on dualities, and belief is fundamentally an act of differentiation. Ideas that create meaning must therefore be conditional beliefs: certain knowledge about God or everlasting life, either way, would destroy their meaning. Reason is destructive of meaning when it seeks unconditional and absolute answers. The ascetic mystic withdraws meaning from the material to put it into the spiritual; the magician sees no vast gulf between them."@en ;
    skos:note "Condition of failure: to question any level of meaning with reason is usually to lose it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Occult Priestcraft, p. 180; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Occult Priestcraft, p. 181; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicReasonAgainstMeaningCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "When reason seeks absolute answers, ask whether reason is out of phase with existence"@en, "Wenn die Vernunft unbedingte Antworten sucht, fragen, ob sie nicht neben dem Dasein liegt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Since reason destroys meaning when it seeks unconditional and absolute answers, the work says it is more prudent, in that context, to stay the hand of suicide and ask whether reason is not somewhat out of phase with the nature of existence."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicMeaningAsMotivation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Occult Priestcraft, p. 181; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicAsceticWithdrawal a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The ascetic mystic's withdrawal of meaning from the material"@en, "Der Rückzug des asketischen Mystikers aus dem Sinn des Stofflichen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The ascetic mystic conceives a vast difference between the material and the spiritual and withdraws meaning from the material in order to put it into the spiritual, seeking indifference to sex, hunger, pleasure and pain, to everything that moves ordinary people."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Occult Priestcraft, p. 181; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicChargedInnerLife a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "A world of spiritual experience: dreams, devotion and inner thought charged with meaning"@en, "Eine Welt geistlicher Erfahrung: Träume, Andacht und inneres Denken mit Sinn geladen"@de ;
    skos:definition "In return for his indifference to everything that motivates ordinary people, a whole world of what the ascetic calls spiritual experiences opens to him: his dreams, his acts of devotion and his inner thoughts become charged with meaning."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Occult Priestcraft, p. 181; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAsceticWithdrawalYieldsChargedInnerLife a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Withdrawing meaning from the material is said to charge dreams, devotion and thought with meaning"@en, "Den Sinn aus dem Stofflichen abzuziehen soll Träume, Andacht und Denken mit Sinn laden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The ascetic's indifference to material motives is said to open a world of spiritual experiences in return, an exercise the work calls bizarre but with an inner logic."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicAsceticWithdrawal ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicChargedInnerLife ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Occult Priestcraft, p. 181; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSpiritMatterContinuum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The magician's continuum from Chaos through the aethers to matter, operating in all spheres"@en, "Das Kontinuum des Magiers vom Chaos über die Äther zur Materie, in allen Bereichen wirkend"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician sees spirit and matter as parts of the same thing and exalts neither; he lives in a continuum from the sublime and ineffable Tao, God or Chaos through the subtle aethers to the strange material world, rejects no part of his experience, and holds any knowledge, power or opportunity for enlightenment worth having for its own sake; the only thing abhorred is failure to come to grips with some part of existence. He should be equally at home with a crozier, a paintbrush, a test tube or a wand; in all things he seeks to bring his Kia into manifestation, life is its own answer, and the way he lives it is his spirituality. Lack of knowledge is for him the ultimate source of meaning, and the true priest is one who can communicate the sense of mystery."@en ;
    skos:note "Religions are said to need a paradoxical or dual highest principle or a fall, to be innately repressive and conservative, to evolve only through heresy and schism, and to exist only as social phenomena of authority and obedience; no religion's text is cited."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Occult Priestcraft, p. 181; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Occult Priestcraft, p. 182; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTemporaryBelief a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Acting without belief, or investing belief temporarily in whatever one experiments with"@en, "Ohne Glauben handeln oder den Glauben zeitweilig in das legen, womit man experimentiert"@de ;
    skos:definition "To be able to operate in all spheres the magician must master the art either of acting without belief or of investing belief temporarily in anything with which he is experimenting."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Occult Priestcraft, p. 182; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicTemporaryBeliefBeforeAllSpheres a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Operating in all spheres presupposes mastery of temporary belief"@en, "In allen Bereichen zu wirken setzt die Beherrschung des zeitweiligen Glaubens voraus"@de ;
    skos:definition "To be able to operate in all spheres, the magician must master acting without belief or investing belief temporarily in what he experiments with."@en ;
    skos:note "Set at prescribed order: the work says must, and states no consequence beyond the implied inability."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicSpiritMatterContinuum ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicTemporaryBelief ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Occult Priestcraft, p. 182; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicGroupIntercession a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Group intercession by an occult priest: total commitment without lust of result"@en, "Gruppenfürbitte durch einen okkulten Priester: völlige Hingabe ohne Gier nach Ergebnis"@de ;
    skos:definition "When the occult priest leads his order or coven in magical activity there is a high probability of failure, because they may not raise sufficient power and may not be doing exactly what is required, and everyone knows it. They must act with total commitment and without the slightest trace of lust of result. Everything possible must first be done on the physical plane to set up the conditions of success, and then the magic is hurled in to tip the balance. To have given one's utmost is enough in itself; the result can be awaited without fear or desire and received with laughter, whatever it may be."@en ;
    skos:note "Two conditions of failure are named: insufficient power raised, and not doing exactly what is required."@en ;
    skos:note "The same page mocks the orthodox catchphrase that a failed intercession was not god's will. The work's own provision for failure is that the result, whatever it is, is to be received with laughter; neither form names an outcome that would count against the practice. Magic is called anarchic: some have a greater gift for it, and sometimes it fails."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Occult Priestcraft, p. 182; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Occult Priestcraft, p. 183; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicBeliefsAlwaysFalse a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ideas may be true, beliefs are always false; beliefs about death are devices for this life"@en, "Ideen können wahr sein, Glaubenssätze sind immer falsch; Glaube an den Tod ist ein Mittel für dieses Leben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Offered as a definition: ideas may be true but beliefs are always false, since what separates a belief from an idea is the emotional force committed to upholding it, and something really true would need no effort to believe. All beliefs about death are improbable and must be conditional, holding both heaven and hell, both pleasant and unpleasant rebirths; an unconditional scheme would be useless and unsatisfying, which shows such beliefs to be mostly devices to create emotional effects in this life. The occult priest should add nothing to this heap but devote his talents to showing people what death is like."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Occult Priestcraft, p. 183; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicNecromancy a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Necromancy: having seen or spoken with the dead"@en, "Nekromantie: die Toten gesehen oder mit ihnen gesprochen haben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Necromancy is called something of a dying art, largely because it has been abused by those who are merely telepathic with the living or after money; yet those who have directly seen or spoken with the dead are said to have a certainty beyond faith. In the paradigm of higher dimensionality necromancy is explained by information moved through a fifth dimension."@en ;
    skos:note "The abuse named, telepathy with the living mistaken for contact with the dead, is the work's own account of how a necromantic result can be counterfeit; it gives no means of telling the two apart."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Occult Priestcraft, p. 183; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Occult Priestcraft, p. 184; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Paradigms, p. 199; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicCultDynamics a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "How cults bind: hierarchy, re-rationalisation through action, licence, secrecy and insignia"@en, "Wie Kulte binden: Hierarchie, Nachrationalisierung durch Handlung, Erlaubnis, Geheimhaltung und Abzeichen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The dynamics of cults, cabals and religions show how magical orders ought and ought not to be organised. In a religion hierarchy is an object of worship; hierarchs present themselves as emissaries of higher powers, which eases submission, and once established the asymmetric relation perpetuates itself: personal comments by the leader, the things friends would not say plus a few one wants to hear, make the guru seem the wisest man on earth. People are persuaded into belief not intellectually but by being made to act, in childhood or under stress, and then adjusting their beliefs; small inconsequential obediences force the rationalisation of loyalty and lead to greater submissions of intelligence, wallet and sexual favours. Leaders license their followers to act without responsibility, overriding inhibitions to violence and sexuality. Cults manufacture secrecy and artificial elites as enticement and control and reinforce identity with dress, badges and labels, so that membership substitutes for self-enlargement; their leaders have mostly swallowed their own lies, which gives them charisma. A cult ends either as a harmless commercial institution or grows more extremist until the state smashes it."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as an account and not as practices or capacities: the work describes these as tricks that a genuine order will spot and ridicule."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Occult Priestcraft, p. 184; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Occult Priestcraft, p. 185; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicGenuineMagicalOrder a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The genuine magical order: every man his own priest"@en, "Der echte magische Orden: jeder sein eigener Priester"@de ;
    skos:definition "A genuine magical order is said to wage psychic guerrilla warfare against all black cults and religions, materialist philosophies included. In it every man is his own priest, any member may teach any other anything he knows, and no member may claim any secret within the order beyond that of his own identity. It admits by quality and not number, any hierarchy reflects demonstrable ability, and the teachers' tricks are spotted and ridiculed at once. Its only justification is to enable individuals to take control of their own spirituality, which is called a heroic and dangerous objective; the chapter ends with a warning to watch out for the police raid."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Occult Priestcraft, p. 185; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Occult Priestcraft, p. 186; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicFiveMagicalWeapons a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The five classes of magical weapon: sword, pentacle, wand, cup and lamp"@en, "Die fünf Klassen magischer Waffen: Schwert, Pentakel, Stab, Kelch und Lampe"@de ;
    skos:definition "Magical weapons fall into five classes by function rather than by physical appearance. All are designed to affect the physical, but the weapons themselves exist primarily on the aetheric or astral level, the physical form being only a convenient handle or anchor. The sword and pentacle are weapons of analysis and synthesis, aetheric weapons through which will, perception and imagination execute mental commands on the planes of middle nature; the wand and cup transmit the power of the life force, Kia, directly onto the aetheric, the wand as the weapon of will and the cup of perception. Of these processes at the interface of consciousness and matter all that can be said is that some seem to proceed inward and some outward; the lesson of all higher ecstasies is that the difference is arbitrary and unreal, and only the powers of the lamp transcend the paradox. The way of the magician is the manifestation of spirit within matter, and his primary technique is gnosis."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Weapons, p. 187; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Weapons, p. 188; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Weapons, p. 189; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPentacle a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The pentacle, weapon of synthesis and altar of lesser pentacles"@en, "Das Pentakel, Waffe der Synthese und Altar kleinerer Pentakel"@de ;
    skos:definition "Upon the pentacle aetheric forms, images and powers are assembled when the magical will and perception vitalise the imagination. The magician may create hundreds of pentacles, yet a general-purpose weapon of this class has virtue, since it can serve as an altar for consecrating lesser pentacles; for many evocations the pentacle is placed on the cup and the conjuration performed with the wand."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Weapons, p. 187; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAccruedWeaponPower a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The power that accrues to a permanent weapon with use"@en, "Die Kraft, die einer dauernden Waffe durch Gebrauch zuwächst"@de ;
    skos:definition "The power of a general-purpose pentacle is said to increase with use, and power accrues likewise to a small, portable and permanent wand."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Weapons, p. 187; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Weapons, p. 188; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPentacleYieldsAccruedPower a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The power of a general-purpose pentacle is said to increase with use"@en, "Die Kraft eines Allzweck-Pentakels soll mit dem Gebrauch wachsen"@de ;
    skos:definition "There is virtue in a general-purpose pentacle because its power increases with use."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPentacle ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicAccruedWeaponPower ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Weapons, p. 187; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicSword a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The sword or dagger, weapon of analysis and undoing"@en, "Schwert oder Dolch, Waffe der Analyse und des Auflösens"@de ;
    skos:definition "The sword, or more usually the dagger, is the weapon of analysis or scission, in the simplest sense destruction: through it the magical will and perception vitalise the imagination of the undoing of things. Like the wand it is manipulated so as to describe vividly to the will and the subconscious what is required of them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Weapons, p. 187; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Weapons, p. 188; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDisintegratingAethericInfluences a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Disintegrating the aetheric influences through which matter is affected"@en, "Die ätherischen Einflüsse auflösen, durch die das Stoffliche bewirkt wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "The sword is called the reservoir of the power that disintegrates aetheric influences through which the material plane is affected."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Weapons, p. 187; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSwordYieldsDisintegration a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The sword is said to hold the power that disintegrates aetheric influences"@en, "Das Schwert soll die Kraft halten, die ätherische Einflüsse auflöst"@de ;
    skos:definition "Through the sword the will and perception vitalise the imagination of the undoing of things, and it is said to be the reservoir of the power that disintegrates aetheric influences."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicSword ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicDisintegratingAethericInfluences ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Weapons, p. 187; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicCup a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The cup, receptacle of magical perception: mirror, shew stone, trance, tarot, dream"@en, "Der Kelch, Gefäß der magischen Wahrnehmung: Spiegel, Schaustein, Trance, Tarot, Traum"@de ;
    skos:definition "The cup is an aetheric receptacle for the magical perception and the weapon least likely to resemble its namesake, though cups of ink or blood are sometimes used; for some it exists as a mirror, a shew stone, a state of trance, a tarot pack, a mandala, a state of dreaming, or a feeling that just comes. These preoccupy the mind so that magical perceptions can surface unhindered by discursive thought and imagination; part of the power built up in them is likened to self-fascination."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Weapons, p. 188; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicCupYieldsDivination a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The cup is said to become an autohypnotic doorway for perception into other realms"@en, "Der Kelch soll zu einer selbsthypnotischen Pforte der Wahrnehmung in andere Bereiche werden"@de ;
    skos:definition "Through self-fascination the cup weapon acquires an autohypnotic quality and is said to provide a doorway through which the perception has access to other realms."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicCup ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicDivinationPsychonaut ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Weapons, p. 188; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicWand a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The wand, projecting the magical will: sigil, amulet, ring, mantra or gesture"@en, "Der Stab, der den magischen Willen aussendet: Sigille, Amulett, Ring, Mantra oder Geste"@de ;
    skos:definition "As an instrument assisting the projection of the magical will onto the aetheric and material planes the wand may be a general-purpose sigil, an amulet, a ring, an enchanting mantra, or even an act or gesture. A small, portable, permanent device of this class has virtue, for power accrues to it with use. Like the cup it fascinates the surface functions of the mind and channels the forces concealed in the depths; like the sword it is manipulated to describe vividly to the will and subconscious what is required of them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Weapons, p. 188; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicWandYieldsAccruedPower a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Power is said to accrue to a permanent wand with use"@en, "Einem dauernden Stab soll mit dem Gebrauch Kraft zuwachsen"@de ;
    skos:definition "There is virtue in a small, portable and permanent wand, for power accrues to it with use."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicWand ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicAccruedWeaponPower ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Weapons, p. 188; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicWandYieldsEnchantment a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The wand is said to assist the projection of the will onto the aetheric and material planes"@en, "Der Stab soll helfen, den Willen auf die ätherische und stoffliche Ebene zu senden"@de ;
    skos:definition "The wand, in whatever form, is said to assist the projection of the magical will onto the aetheric and material planes by fascinating the surface mind and channelling the forces of the depths."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicWand ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicEnchantmentPsychonaut ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Weapons, p. 188; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicLamp a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The lamp, which channels Chaos and Kia and inspirits the magician"@en, "Die Lampe, die Chaos und Kia leitet und den Magier begeistet"@de ;
    skos:definition "The lamp is so named only from the popular analogy of spirit with light, for Chaos and Kia are as likely to be felt as an awesome darkness, or as brilliance and voidness at once. As a device to channel these forces to the magician's mundane consciousness it may take any form, from an idea of God or the Tao to a primitive-looking fetish or symbol. It should aid his gnosis and receive the forces he generates; it is the weapon of inspiration in the original sense, which inspirits him."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Weapons, p. 188; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Weapons, p. 189; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicLampYieldsGnosticLevel a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The lamp is said to aid gnosis and inspirit the magician"@en, "Die Lampe soll die Gnosis unterstützen und den Magier begeisten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician's lamp is something that aids his gnosis and receives the forces he generates; as the weapon of inspiration it inspirits him."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicLamp ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicGnosticLevel ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Weapons, p. 189; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicEmptyHandTechnique a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Empty-hand technique: ritual on the astral, visualising the weapons until they are hallucinated"@en, "Technik der leeren Hand: Ritual auf der Astralebene, die Waffen visualisieren, bis man sie halluziniert"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician should be able to perform any ritual on the astral, by the power of imagination alone, strongly visualising any of his weapons to the point where he actually hallucinates their presence."@en ;
    skos:note "Sign of success: the visualised weapon is actually hallucinated as present. Such empty-hand techniques are called the mark of an adept."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Weapons, p. 189; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAstralRitual a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Performing any ritual by imagination alone, the weapons' aetheric forms drawn into action"@en, "Jedes Ritual allein durch Vorstellung vollziehen, die ätherischen Formen der Waffen in Tätigkeit gezogen"@de ;
    skos:definition "By visualising a weapon until its presence is hallucinated, the magician is said to draw both the aetheric form of the weapon and the associated powers within himself into action, so that any ritual can be performed on the astral without physical instruments."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Weapons, p. 189; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicEmptyHandTechniqueYieldsAstralRitual a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Hallucinating one's weapons is said to set their aetheric forms and one's own powers to work"@en, "Die eigenen Waffen zu halluzinieren soll ihre ätherischen Formen und die eigenen Kräfte in Gang setzen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Strong visualisation of the weapon to the point of hallucination is said to draw its aetheric form and the associated powers within the magician into action."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicEmptyHandTechnique ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicAstralRitual ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Weapons, p. 189; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMagicalParadigms a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Magical paradigms: the universe confirms whichever paradigm one lives in"@en, "Magische Paradigmen: das Universum bestätigt jedes Paradigma, in dem man lebt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Every system of thought rests on postulates that make up the paradigm through which a culture or individual meets its universe; aeons are marked by the passage of great metaphysical paradigms, and the dominant Western ones are called Protestant atheism, with liberal individualism and the work ethic, and science, with causality and materialism. The universe, being accommodating, is said to provide confirmation of any paradigm one chooses to live in; we are to an extent in an observer-created universe. A modern magical view must encompass the scientific paradigm to be effective in a technological civilisation. All magical paradigms involve action at a distance in space, time or both, which in magic is called synchronicity: a mental event, perception or act of will occurs at the same time as an event in the material world. This can always be excused as coincidence, but most magicians would be quite content to be able to arrange coincidences. Six paradigms follow: chaoetheric, probability manipulation, morphic fields, observer-created universe, holographic universe, and higher dimensionality."@en ;
    skos:note "Two statements of this section bear on testing. A universe that confirms any paradigm lived in confirms the magical one as it does its rivals; and an effect that can always be excused as coincidence is accepted by the work as the effect sought, so that a result and a coincidence are not distinguished."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Paradigms, p. 191; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Paradigms, p. 192; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicChaoethericParadigm a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The chaoetheric paradigm: an island of order in an ocean of chaos, and the aethers"@en, "Das chaoätherische Paradigma: eine Insel der Ordnung in einem Meer aus Chaos, und die Äther"@de ;
    skos:definition "The manifest universe is a tiny island of comparative order in an infinite ocean of primal chaos or potentia, which pervades every interstice of it; the island was randomly spewed up and will be redissolved, improbable but bound to occur. We are its most highly ordered structure, yet at the centre of our being is a spark of the same chaos, which animates us and lets us work magic. Chaos cannot be perceived directly, since it contains the opposite of anything we think it is, but we can glimpse and use partially formed matter of probabilistic and indeterminate existence, called the aethers. There are two schools in magic: one holds the formative agent of the universe random and chaotic, the other a spiritual consciousness, each speaking of its own nature; the author inclines to the view that his spiritual consciousness is random and chaotic in an agreeable way."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Paradigms, p. 192; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Paradigms, p. 193; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicProbabilityManipulation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Probability manipulation: engineering coincidences at the point where an event is still uncertain"@en, "Wahrscheinlichkeitslenkung: Zufälle an dem Punkt herbeiführen, an dem ein Ereignis noch unbestimmt ist"@de ;
    skos:definition "A more modest version of the first paradigm: somewhere in the genesis of every event there is a point at which its future reality is uncertain, a level of disorganisation, given mathematical form by the Heisenberg uncertainty principle, at which the universe does not know what it is about to do. Events in the mind share this quality. A hidden variable deciding which possibility materialises is suspected to be consciousness or information. Armed with this idea and applying his magic to the critical point, the magician is said to be able to engineer some impressive coincidences."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Paradigms, p. 193; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMorphicFields a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Morphic fields: every event predisposes its like, and every species has its egregore"@en, "Morphische Felder: jedes Ereignis bahnt seinesgleichen, und jede Art hat ihren Egregor"@de ;
    skos:definition "The hypothesis of formative causation is taken as an excellent magical paradigm: whenever a new event occurs it generates a morphic field that predisposes all later similar events, everywhere and at any time, to occur the same way. Clairvoyance is then the tapping of a morphic field left by a past event, and atavisms, entities, gods and demons are the fields left by animals and human thoughts; if imagining an event generates a small field, the effectiveness of visualisation is explained. Only prophecy, always the most doubtful of the mantic arts, cannot be fitted in. Against religion, which puts consciousness before life, and science, which puts life first, magic holds that organic and psychic forms evolve together, each species generating a psychic form or egregore that feeds back into it; those who have perceived the human egregore usually describe it as God, and all life on earth contributes to a vast composite egregore known as the Great Mother, Anima Mundi, Great Archon, Devil, Pan and Baphomet."@en ;
    skos:note "The hypothesis is cited without author or title. Sign of success: an egregore may be felt as a presence or even glimpsed in the form of the species it watches over."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Paradigms, p. 193; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Paradigms, p. 194; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicSympatheticMagic a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Sympathetic magic: representing an event in miniature"@en, "Sympathetische Magie: ein Ereignis im Kleinen darstellen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sympathetic magic becomes the deliberate representation of an event in miniature to produce a morphic field that causes the desired event to occur elsewhere."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Paradigms, p. 194; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSympatheticMagicYieldsEnchantment a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "A representation in miniature is said to make the desired event occur elsewhere"@en, "Eine Darstellung im Kleinen soll das gewünschte Ereignis anderswo eintreten lassen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The miniature representation of an event is said to produce a morphic field that causes the desired event to occur elsewhere."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicSympatheticMagic ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicEnchantmentPsychonaut ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Paradigms, p. 194; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmp:ChaosMagicCommunionWithBeasts a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Communion with the morphic field of a species"@en, "Gemeinschaft mit dem morphischen Feld einer Art"@de ;
    skos:definition "Communion with the morphic fields of beasts is called of great importance to the shaman and sorcerer; the work offers it as the reason for the worldwide occurrence of totemism among hunting peoples and for the animal-headed, human-bodied gods of most mythologies."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Paradigms, p. 194; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPowerOverSpecies a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Knowledge of a species, power over it, and appropriation of its powers"@en, "Kenntnis einer Art, Macht über sie und Aneignung ihrer Kräfte"@de ;
    skos:definition "Communion with the field of a species conveys intimate knowledge of the creature, allows the magician a certain power over the species, and may let him appropriate some of the beast's powers, particularly on the aetheric plane."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Paradigms, p. 194; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicCommunionWithBeastsYieldsPowerOverSpecies a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Communion with a species' field is said to give knowledge of it, power over it and some of its powers"@en, "Gemeinschaft mit dem Feld einer Art soll Kenntnis von ihr, Macht über sie und einen Teil ihrer Kräfte geben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Communion with the morphic field of a beast is said to convey intimate knowledge of the creature, a certain power over the species, and some of its powers, especially on the aetheric plane."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicCommunionWithBeasts ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPowerOverSpecies ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Paradigms, p. 194; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAnimalAtavism a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Taking on an animal atavism: the karma of a cat"@en, "Einen tierischen Atavismus annehmen: das Karma einer Katze"@de ;
    skos:definition "In the anecdotes the author made a sigil to acquire the karma of a cat; having concentrated on it for a while without result, he had more or less forgotten it when, days later in a poorly lit alley, his eyes met those of a large alley cat on a gatepost, something indescribable flashed between them, and he tore off into the darkness howling and screeching like a feline, completely possessed; only the lack of grime on his hands afterwards convinced him he had not been on all fours. Gnosis directed by beast-form visualisation and sigils is said to reach the subconscious behaviour programmes of the older brain."@en ;
    skos:note "Sign of success in the case: the flash between the eyes and the sudden possession. The sigil gave no result when first concentrated on and is credited with the possession days later; the work sets no interval within which a result would count."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Anecdotes, p. 203; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page", "Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut (York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987; fifth printing, 1997), Psychonaut, Magical Perspectives, Baphomet, p. 161; read in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page" .

